on': [{'firstname': 'Phoebe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/efb51466-8608-553b-ae98-e2896c416c24', 'word_count': 1255, 'uri': 'nyt://article/efb51466-8608-553b-ae98-e2896c416c24'}, {'abstract': 'We’re open sourcing our video transcoding API and video encoding presets, as part of our plan to open source our video encoding and distribution pipeline.', 'web_url': 'https://open.blogs.nytimes.com/2016/11/01/using-microservices-to-encode-and-publish-videos-at-the-new-york-times/', 'snippet': 'We’re open sourcing our video transcoding API and video encoding presets, as part of our plan to open source our video encoding and distribution pipeline.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Video publishing at The Times is growingFor the past 10 years, the video publishing lifecycle at The New York Times has relied on vendors and in-house hardware solutions. With our growing investment in video journalism over the past couple of years, we’ve found ourselves producing more video content every month, along with supporting new initiatives such as 360-degree video and Virtual Reality. This growth has created the need to migrate to a video publishing platform that could adapt to, and keep up with, the fast pace that our newsroom demands and the continued evolution of our production process. Along with this, we needed a system that could continuously scale in both capacity and features while not compromising on either quality or reliability.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Using Microservices to Encode and Publish Videos at The New York Times', 'kicker': 'Open', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Open-Source Software', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Software', 'rank': 2, '