asquerade ball.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/01/fashion/halloween-costumes-for-a-cause-parties.html', 'snippet': 'Bette Midler hosts the Hulaween Bash, MoMA P.S.1 hosts “White House of Terror,” and the Harlem School of the Arts holds a masquerade ball.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Costumes aside, Halloween offered philanthropic causes a festive way to raise money and awareness. Bette Midler brought back her “Hocus Pocus” character to life for her annual Hulaween Bash on Oct. 28, which benefits the New York Restoration Project. MoMA P.S. 1 and Susanne Bartsch (center, in red cape) held its fifth annual Halloween party on Oct. 29. And the Harlem School of the Arts held a masquerade ball on Oct. 31 at the Plaza Hotel.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Costumes for a Cause', 'kicker': 'Party Coverage: Scene City', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Halloween', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parties (Social)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Midler, Bette', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Harlem School of the Arts', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'MoMA PS1', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-01T21:06:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Denny Lee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Denny', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e0188940-6bd5-53b1-ac6b-cc89a582b9a4', 'word_count': 76, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e0188940-6bd5-53b1-ac6b-cc89a582b9a4'}, {'abstract': 'A teenager from a nomadic family bucks gender norms by raising a bird and training it to hunt in this documentary.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/02/movies/the-eagle-huntress-review.html', 'snippet': 'A teenager from a nomadic family bucks gender norms by raising a bird and training it to hunt in this documentary.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Aisholpan is a 13-year-old girl who lives in Mongolia, dividing her time between a boarding school and her nomadic family’s campsite. With the release of “The Eagle Huntress,” a thrilling new documentary directed by Otto Bell, she may well become something else: a pop-culture heroine with the power to inspire girls (and not only girls) everywhere. It would not be surprising on future Halloweens to spot a handful of Aisholpans, dressed in traditional fur-and-embroidery hunting gear, pigtails fastened with pink bows, amid the throngs of Elsas and Katnisses.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/02/arts/02EAGLE2/02EAGLE2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/02/arts/02EAGLE2/02EAGLE2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/02/arts/02EAGLE2/02EAGLE2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/02/arts/02EAGLE2/02EAGLE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 317, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/02/arts/02EAGLE2/02EAGLE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 317}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/02/arts/02EAGLE2/02EAGLE2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 541, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/02/arts/02EAGLE2/02EAGLE2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1081, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: In ‘The Eagle Huntress,’ a Girl From Mongolia Soars', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Central Asia, a Girl and Her Raptor Soar to Spectacular Heights', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Eagle Huntress (Movie)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nurgaiv, Aisholpan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bell, Otto', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Furler, Sia', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-01T21:08:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/bdd9cc4d-6c80-57cd-bde8-fa2d401d33e2', 'word_count': 590, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bdd9cc4d-6c80-57cd-bde8-fa2d401d33e2'}, {'abstract': 'Kenneth MacMillan’s ballet, performed by the Royal Ballet with Natalia Osipova, revolves around a woman who claimed to be the surviving daughter of Czar Nicholas II.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/02/arts/dance/review-anastasia-royal-ballet-grand-duchess-or-a-great-pretender.html', 'snippet': 'Kenneth MacMillan’s ballet, performed by the Royal Ballet with Natalia Osipova, revolves around a woman who claimed to be the surviving daughter of Czar Nicholas II.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Royalty, revolution, madness, memory. These are the potentially explosive ingredients of the choreographer Kenneth MacMillan’s “Anastasia,” his 1971 full-length ballet currently being performed by the Royal Ballet here for the first time since 2004, and offering the enticing prospect of the Russian ballerina Natalia Osipova making her debut in the title role.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/02/arts/02ANASTASIA2/02ANASTASIA2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/02/arts/02ANASTASIA2/02ANASTASIA2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/02/arts/02ANASTASIA2/02ANASTASIA2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/02/arts/02ANASTASIA2/02ANASTASIA2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 761, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/02/arts/02ANASTASIA2/02ANASTASIA2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 761}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/02/arts/02ANASTASIA2/02ANASTASIA2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 808, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/02/arts/02ANASTASIA2/02ANASTASIA2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1615, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: ‘Anastasia,’ a Grand Duchess or a Great Pretender?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Is She a Grand Duchess or a Great Pretender?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Anastasia (Ballet)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sulcas, Roslyn', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Royal Ballet', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'MacMillan, Kenneth', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Osipova, Natalia', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-01T21:14:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roslyn Sulcas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roslyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sulcas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d365d367-00c1-524a-9b25-be1106a78d46', 'word_count': 617, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d365d367-00c1-524a-9b25-be1106a78d46'}, {'abstract': 'In this Brazilian film from Anna Muylaert, an androgynous teenager discovers that the woman who raised him isn’t his biological mother.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/02/movies/dont-call-me-son-review-anna-muylaert.html', 'snippet': 'In this Brazilian film from Anna Muylaert, an androgynous teenager discovers that the woman who raised him isn’t his biological mother.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Who are you? That question nags at Pierre (Naomi Nero), the brooding androgynous protagonist of Anna Muylaert’s “Don’t Call Me Son.” This Brazilian film’s title takes on a double meaning after Pierre discovers that Arcay (Dani Nefussi), the woman he presumed to be his biological mother, stole him from the hospital when he was a baby. That upsetting news arrives when the authorities burst into their shabby apartment, slap handcuffs on Arcay, then immediately subject her and Pierre to DNA tests.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/02/arts/02DONTCALL/02DONTCALL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/02/arts/02DONTCALL/02DONTCALL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/02/arts/02DONTCALL/02DONTCALL-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/02/arts/02DONTCALL/02DONTCALL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 337, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/02/arts/02DONTCALL/02DONTCALL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 337}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/02/arts/02DONTCALL/02DONTCALL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/02/arts/02DONTCALL/02DONTCALL-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1152, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: ‘Don’t Call Me Son’ Considers Identity and Fluidity', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Identity and Fluidity Reconsidered', 'name': None, 'seo': N