ifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}, {'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': 'K.', 'lastname': 'Rashbaum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 4}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/26f2596b-1631-57c4-bb7d-727e1be753af', 'word_count': 1160, 'uri': 'nyt://article/26f2596b-1631-57c4-bb7d-727e1be753af'}, {'abstract': 'As more people are arrested and media outlets closed, other countries need to speak the bitter truth to Turkey.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/02/opinion/can-turkeys-democracy-survive-president-erdogan.html', 'snippet': 'As more people are arrested and media outlets closed, other countries need to speak the bitter truth to Turkey.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What is unnerving in President Recep Tayyip Erdogan’s march to authoritarianism is how dismally familiar it is: the coup that becomes a pretext for a massive roundup of real and imagined enemies; the claims to be the one man who can withstand the onslaught of foreign foes; the invocation of purported historical slights; the silencing of the news media. The world has seen this before in other countries. The pattern is tried and true; the tough question is how to break it.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/02/opinion/02wed3web/02wed3web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/02/opinion/02wed3web/02wed3web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/02/opinion/02wed3web/02wed3web-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtyp