one, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9e4161e6-c016-5665-849d-f4c8198d33f7', 'word_count': 907, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9e4161e6-c016-5665-849d-f4c8198d33f7'}, {'abstract': 'The host of “The Daily Show” on his childhood in apartheid-era South Africa and getting comfortable in Jon Stewart’s chair.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/06/magazine/trevor-noah-wasnt-expecting-liberal-hatred.html', 'snippet': 'The host of “The Daily Show” on his childhood in apartheid-era South Africa and getting comfortable in Jon Stewart’s chair.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Your memoir, “Born a Crime,” is a striking depiction of your life in South Africa both under and after apartheid. How has that experience formed your perspective on the divisions we’re seeing in America because of the election? America is the place that always seems to treat the symptoms and not the cause. In South Africa, we’re very good at trying to go for the cause of racism. One thing that really never happened here, which is strange to me, was a period where white America had to reconcile with what it had done to black Americans.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '58', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/06/magazine/06talk/06talk-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 1427, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/06/magazine/06talk/06talk-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1427}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/06/magazine/06talk/06talk-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 431, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/06/ma