ified take on a Chinese takeout dish that became a New York restaurant classic.', 'lead_paragraph': '“It’s just a complete rip-off of mapo tofu,” the chef Tien Ho said. Ho was once David Chang’s chief lieutenant at Momofuku Ssam Bar in New York, and he was talking about the origins of one of the restaurant’s greatest hits: spicy pork sausage and rice cakes, born in 2006. “We would always order mapo tofu from that Sichuan place on St. Mark’s, after work,” he said, “and Chang thought we could make something like it for the restaurant.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/06/magazine/06eat-copy/06mag-06eat-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/06/magazine/06eat-copy/06mag-06eat-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/06/magazine/06eat-copy/06mag-06eat-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/06/magazine/06eat-copy/06mag-06eat-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1536, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/06/magazine/06eat-copy/06mag-06eat-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/06/magazine/06eat-copy/06mag-06eat-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/06/magazine/06eat-copy/06mag-06eat-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Not All Ragùs Are Italian', 'kicker': 'Eat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Not All Ragùs Are Italian', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chinese Food (Cuisine)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ho, Tien', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Whole Foods Market Inc', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chang, David (Chef)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-03T09:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Sifton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sifton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/66182ae4-701f-5c81-a07b-ce370ddd94ca', 'word_count': 710, 'uri': 'nyt://article/66182ae4-701f-5c81-a07b-ce370ddd94ca'}, {'abstract': 'It’s typically insulting to call a show formulaic, but sometimes the genius is the formula.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/06/magazine/letter-of-recommendation-pinky-and-the-brain.html', 'snippet': 'It’s typically insulting to call a show formulaic, but sometimes the genius is the formula.', 'lead_paragraph': 'During an interview I did with Conan O’Brien a while back, we drifted onto the topic of rules: when it was prudent, as a creative person, to flout conventions, and when it made sense to obey them. As O’Brien saw it, only ever flouting, without a healthy dose of obeying, was unsustainable and, on balance, unfunny. To illustrate, he told me a story, one that didn’t make it into the piece I wrote but stuck with me nonetheless: A well-known comedian with a raucous persona, interested in developing his own talk show, once sought O’Brien’s advice. “He showed me a prototype of what it would be: He comes out, he runs through the audience, he’s never in one place. He’s everywhere, he’s nowhere, he’s wearing different clothes, spray-painting on the walls — total anarchy,” O’Brien recalled. “And I said, O.K., here’s my take: You’re a brilliantly funny person, but you need to be constrained, because when you break those constraints, it’s enormously satisfying.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/06/magazine/06lor-copy/06mag-06lor-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 427, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/06/magazine/06lor-copy/06mag-06lor-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 427}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/06/magazine/06lor-copy/06mag-06lor-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 729, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/06/magazine/06lor-copy/06mag-06lor-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1457, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/06/magazine/06lor-copy/06mag-06lor-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/06/magazine/06lor-copy/06mag-06lor-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/06/magazine/06lor-copy/06mag-06lor-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Letter of Recommendation: ‘Pinky and the Brain’', 'kicker': 'Letter of Recommendation', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Pinky and the Brain’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weiner, Jonah', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comedy and Humor', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Animaniacs (TV Program)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': "O'Brien, Conan", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Pinky and the Brain (TV Program)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-03T09:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonah Weiner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weiner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d305f5ae-4af4-5e9b-9529-3a63ee05ca0e', 'word_count': 902, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d305f5ae-4af4-5e9b-9529-3a63ee05ca0e'}, {'abstract': 'Should the right to free speech trump our desire to end the use of hateful words?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/03/learning/do-laws-that-ban-offensive-words-make-the-world-a-better-place.html', 'snippet': 'Should the right to free speech trump our desire to end the use of hateful words?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lawmakers in South Africa want to make hate speech a crime. Backers of the draft law say racist language can violate human rights and is not compatible with the nation’s values. Opponents say that criminalizing hate speech will erode freedom of expression and do little to heal the country’s racial wounds.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Do Laws That Ban Offensive Words Make the World a Better Place?', 'kicker': 'Student Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2016-11-03T09:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Learning', 'section_name': 'The Learning Network', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Gonchar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gonchar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5d000701-c60e-5d0d-93b9-a34691ff4448', 'word_count': 356, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5d000701-c60e-5d0d-93b9-a34691ff4448'}, {'abstract': 'Two decades ago, in a land of sensory overload and spiritual abundance, a doctor mixed together meditation and hilarity.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2016/11/06/magazine/voyages-laughter-yoga-bangalore.html', 'snippet': 'Two decades ago, in a land of sensory overload and spiritual abundance, a doctor mixed together meditation and hilarity.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two decades ago, in a land of sensory overload and spiritual abundance, a doctor mixed together meditation and hilarity.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/06/magazine/06-voyages-india-slide-YQWN/06-voyages-india-slide-YQWN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/06/magazine/06-voyages-india-slide-YQWN/06-voyages-india-slide-YQWN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumb