is host.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Good, Bad and Mad: Andrew Rosenthal on the News', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2016-11-03T20:38:13+0000', 'document_type': 'audio', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Times Insider', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://audio/60497f95-9b53-58fc-a7bb-d6abceb3f730', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://audio/60497f95-9b53-58fc-a7bb-d6abceb3f730'}, {'abstract': 'An exhibition of centuries-old beads, spheres and pendants, with elaborately carved tableaus, will travel from Toronto to New York to Amsterdam.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/04/arts/design/unlocking-the-secrets-of-boxwood-miniatures.html', 'snippet': 'An exhibition of centuries-old beads, spheres and pendants, with elaborately carved tableaus, will travel from Toronto to New York to Amsterdam.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dutch woodcarvers once sculpted slivers of boxwood into biblical scenes smaller than walnut shells, and for a few decades in the 1400s and 1500s, elite clients, including Henry VIII, vied to own the miniature works. Yet no one seems to have recorded much about the sculptors’ techniques or even their names. In notes from the 1630s about an antique boxwood sphere with portraits of martyred saints, a Dutch art collector lamented, “It is regrettable that the maker of this ingenious piece has not made himself known with any sign.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/04/arts/04ANTIQUES1/04ANTIQUES1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/04/arts/04ANTIQUES1/04ANTIQUES1-thumbStandard.jpg', 't