bject', 'value': 'Social Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Anxiety and Stress', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANGER (EMOTION)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-04T07:21:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Dalai Lama and Arthur C. Brooks', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Dalai Lama'}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/81ae61df-d78b-559d-b9bf-2b09487f7842', 'word_count': 837, 'uri': 'nyt://article/81ae61df-d78b-559d-b9bf-2b09487f7842'}, {'abstract': 'The impact of third-party candidates on federal and state elections is as significant as it is overlooked.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/04/opinion/campaign-stops/gary-johnson-queen-maker.html', 'snippet': 'The impact of third-party candidates on federal and state elections is as significant as it is overlooked.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With just a few days before Election Day and all eyes fixed on the two leading candidates, it’s easy to overlook a remarkable fact: The Libertarian candidate for president, Gary Johnson, may tilt the election for Hillary Clinton. And if he does, it wouldn’t be the first time — not even close. The impact of third-party candidates on federal and state elections is as significant as it is overlooked.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Gary Johnson, Queen Maker', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Third-Party Politics (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2016', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johnson, Gary E', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Libertarianism (US Politics)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clinton, Hillary Rodham', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nader, Ralph', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-04T07:21:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Campaign Stops', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lawrence R. Jacobs and Jonathan Spiegler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lawrence', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Jacobs', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Spiegler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/1d2753ed-43a0-544d-8f3b-73732c65f30b', 'word_count': 759, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1d2753ed-43a0-544d-8f3b-73732c65f30b'}, {'abstract': 'By now you’ve heard about Justin Timberlake’s photograph at his polling station. Was he wrong to snap the pic?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/roomfordebate/2016/11/04/are-voting-booth-selfies-fun-or-dangerous', 'snippet': 'By now you’ve heard about Justin Timberlake’s photograph at his polling station. Was he wrong to snap the pic?', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/03/opinion/RFD-voting/RFD-voting-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/03/opinion/RFD-voting/RFD-voting-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/03/opinion/RFD-voting/RFD-voting-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {