_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Baker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Baker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d2bcbcff-8e06-5a53-b3ea-bde4475a6d69', 'word_count': 689, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d2bcbcff-8e06-5a53-b3ea-bde4475a6d69'}, {'abstract': 'From the archives of The International Herald Tribune: A British flotilla flees the Batavier Line steamer Oldambt, a Dutch ship, from a German fleet intent on taking it to Zeebrugge, in November 1916.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2016/11/04/1916-british-flotilla-frees-dutch-ship-from-pirate-fleet/', 'snippet': 'From the archives of The International Herald Tribune: A British flotilla flees the Batavier Line steamer Oldambt, a Dutch ship, from a German fleet intent on taking it to Zeebrugge, in November 1916.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — A despatch from Amsterdam announces that the Batavier Line steamer Oldambt, which left Rotterdam for London on Wednesday evening, was stopped outside the Dutch territorial limit by a fleet of German torpedo boats and submarines. The Germans placed a crew on board the Oldambt for the purpose of taking the steamer into Zeebrugge, but before they could get under way a flotilla of British torpedo boats steamed up, whereupon the Germans took flight, having first placed bombs in the engine-room of the Oldambt. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/04/blogs/04oldiht-1916-pirates/04oldiht-1916-pirates-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 370, 'width': 386, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'imag