', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lasky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2e0e2a69-ae0a-57e9-8e69-8d9347c3153d', 'word_count': 1019, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2e0e2a69-ae0a-57e9-8e69-8d9347c3153d'}, {'abstract': 'In a Manhattan bar crawl, the reality TV stars fixed drinks and provided a real-life diversion from a stressful world.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/05/style/vanderpump-rules-pub-crawl-manhattan.html', 'snippet': 'In a Manhattan bar crawl, the reality TV stars fixed drinks and provided a real-life diversion from a stressful world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the middle of an anxiety-inducing World Series Game 7, six days out from a presidential election, a bar crawl in New York City offered a brief glimpse into an alternate universe governed by some very well-known bartenders.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Vanderpump Rules’ Cast Pours It On During a Bar Crawl', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Vanderpump Rules (TV Program)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reality Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bars and Nightclubs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-04T16:39:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katie Rogers', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rogers', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/90d8ddea-21fd-581f-9aea-d0f445204605', 'word_count': 833, 'uri': 'nyt://article/90d8ddea-21fd-581f-9aea-d0f445204605'}, {'abstract': 'Angela Mao was as famous as Bruce Lee when she was a martial arts film star during the 1970s. Then she seemed to vanish. It turns out she’s been in Queens this whole time.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/06/nyregion/searching-for-lady-kung-fu.html', 'snippet': 'Angela Mao was as famous as Bruce Lee when she was a martial arts film star during the 1970s. Then she seemed to vanish. It turns out she’s been in Queens this whole time.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At the reception for an Asian film festival at Lincoln Center six years ago, excitement rippled through the crowd: Was it her? Lady Kung Fu? Was that Lady Whirlwind?', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/06/nyregion/06KUNG-FU1-sub/06KUNG-FU1-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 851, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/06/nyregion/06KUNG-FU1-sub/06KUNG-FU1-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 851}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/06/nyregion/06KUNG-FU1-sub/06KUNG-FU1-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 723, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/06/nyregion/06KUNG-FU1-sub/06KUNG-FU1-sub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1445, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/06/nyregion/06KUNG-FU1-sub/06KUNG-FU1-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/06/nyregion/06KUNG-FU1-sub/06KUNG-FU1-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/06/nyregion/06KUNG-FU1-sub/06KUNG-FU1-sub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Searching for Lady Kung Fu', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Searching for Lady Kung Fu', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vadukul, Alex', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Martial Arts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mao, Angela (1950- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACTORS AND ACTRESSES', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lee, Bruce', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Queens (NYC)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-04T16:40:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metropolitan', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alex Vadukul', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alex', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vadukul', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e8ca5758-d2a9-565e-9521-c9ebfe7d209c', 'word_count': 2287, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e8ca5758-d2a9-565e-9521-c9ebfe7d209c'}, {'abstract': 'Pat Conroy’s “A Lowcountry Heart,” a posthumous essay collection that includes some material from his charmingly capricious blog, is No. 11 on the hardcover nonfiction list.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/13/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Pat Conroy’s “A Lowcountry Heart,” a posthumous essay collection that includes some material from his charmingly capricious blog, is No. 11 on the hardcover nonfiction list.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A Lesser Tribe: For a few years before he died of pancreatic cancer last March, the novelist Pat Conroy maintained a charmingly capricious blog on his website. Fans may recognize some of its contents in “A Lowcountry Heart,” a posthumous essay collection new at No. 11 in hardcover nonfiction, including a tribute to the writer Ann Patchett that takes the long way to its destination. Here’s a sample detour, about physical beauty in the publishing world: “Late in my time at Houghton Mifflin, two young women arrived on the scene who were the talk of the company — Susan Minot with a novel called ‘Monkeys’ and Ann Patchett with one called ‘The Patron Saint of Liars.’ Both were talented, all agreed, but both were also drop dead beautiful.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '46', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/06/books/review/06conroy/06conroy-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/06/books/review/06conroy/06conroy-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/06/books/review/06conroy/06conroy-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/06/books/review/06conroy/06conroy-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 269, 'width': 256, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/06/books/review/06conroy/06conroy-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 269, 'width': 256, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Story Behind This Week’s Best Sellers', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Conroy, Pat', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-04T16:40:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7ca2fe64-6022-5933-a2c7-30f3ceb5e385', 'word_count': 482, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7ca2fe64-6022-5933-a2c7-30f3ceb5e385'}, {'abstract': 'Michael Connelly continues to think up creative ways to keep his old war horse Harry Bosch on the job.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/06/books/review/michael-connelly-wrong-side-of-goodybe.html', 'snippet': 'Michael Connelly continues to think up creative ways to keep his old war horse Harry Bosch on the job.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sooner or later, Harry Bosch will have to turn in his gun and retire from police work, but until that time Michael Connelly continues to think up creative ways to keep his old war horse on the job. In THE WRONG SIDE OF GOODBYE (Little, Brown, $29), Bosch is doing part-time volunteer work for the undermanned police force of San Fernando, an independent town completely surrounded by the city of Los Angeles. “Look at me,” he remarks, “scratching and fighting to keep a badge on my belt at my age.” The work isn’t too taxing, but in going through several years’ worth of reports on sexual assaults, he notes a pattern common to four of these unsolved cases, which can only mean that a serial rapist is on the loose. And despite a bland sobriquet (“the Screen Cutter”) that makes him sound like the village handyman, this predator is likely to become ever more violent.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '29', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/06/books/review/1106-BKS-Crime/1106-BKS-Crime-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 801, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/06/books/review/1106-BKS-Crime/1106-BKS-Crime-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 801}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/06/books/review/1106-BKS-Crime/1106-BKS-Crime-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 767, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/06/books/review/1106-BKS-Crime/1106-BKS-Crime-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1636, 'width': 1225, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/06/books/review/1106-BKS-Crime/1106-BKS-Crime-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/06/books/review/1106-BKS-Crime/1106-BKS-Crime-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/06/books/review/1106-BKS-Crime/1106-BKS-Crime-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Latest and Best in Crime Fiction', 'kicker':