b_date': '2016-11-05T02:03:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/77367e72-1875-50d8-9de8-170a1f64d6a2', 'word_count': 492, 'uri': 'nyt://article/77367e72-1875-50d8-9de8-170a1f64d6a2'}, {'abstract': 'Donald Trump has made it difficult to avoid describing his behavior as racist.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/05/opinion/the-election-that-obliterated-euphemisms.html', 'snippet': 'Donald Trump has made it difficult to avoid describing his behavior as racist.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Donald Trump campaign inadvertently performed a public service when it exposed the weakness and vulnerability of the euphemisms long used by political journalists. News organizations have been forced to acknowledge that phrases like “stretched the truth” and “fudged the facts” are useless for describing a candidate who speaks falsely in virtually every breath. Genteel circumlocution has given way to calling out lies as lies.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/05/opinion/05sat3web/05sat3web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/05/opinion/05sat3web/05sat3web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/05/opinion/05sat3web/05sat3web-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/05/opinion/05sat3web/05sat3web-