y on Nov. 9', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2016', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clinton, Hillary Rodham', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-05T18:30:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/11b041ab-7397-5b2e-8129-bb9b57c870df', 'word_count': 674, 'uri': 'nyt://article/11b041ab-7397-5b2e-8129-bb9b57c870df'}, {'abstract': 'There is a salve for broken politics, bitter voters and bad choices: royalty.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/06/opinion/sunday/consider-a-monarchy-america.html', 'snippet': 'There is a salve for broken politics, bitter voters and bad choices: royalty.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Southmoor, England — As a foreigner with dual British and Russian citizenship, it is not for me to comment at length on the merits of the rival candidates for the presidency of the United States. But it seems uncontroversial to say that neither appears to be a Washington or a Lincoln, and that the elective presidency is coming under increasingly critical examination.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/06/opinion/sunday/06tolstoy/06tolstoy-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 413, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/06/opinion/sunday/06tolstoy/06tolstoy-articleLarge.jpg', 'x