7f'}, {'abstract': '“On the Inside,” curated by Tatiana von Fürstenberg, was a labor of love intended to empower one of society’s most marginalized communities.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/07/t-magazine/art/tatiana-von-furstenberg-artists-prison.html', 'snippet': '“On the Inside,” curated by Tatiana von Fürstenberg, was a labor of love intended to empower one of society’s most marginalized communities.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I made a pledge to do an act of love every day for 30 days between Martin Luther King Day and Valentine’s Day of 2012. It was a simple online pledge — it had to be an act of love not for friends and family,” explains the filmmaker and musician Tatiana von Fürstenberg. It was this promise that ultimately led her to create an arts initiative empowering LGBTQ prisoners — people who, as von Fürstenberg notes, “discovered art on their own and have been making art, sometimes before they were incarcerated and sometimes after they were incarcerated.” “On the Inside,” the first group show of prisoner work she’s curated through the initiative by prisoners around the country, is on view now at Abrons Arts Center.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/07/t-magazine/art/Prisoner-art-slide-OYU9/Prisoner-art-slide-OYU9-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/07/t-magazine/art/Prisoner-art-slide-OYU9/Prisoner-art-slide-OYU9-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/07/t-magazine/art/Prisoner-art-slide-OYU9/Prisoner-art-slide-OYU9-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'cred