e': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c9b9ff2f-7fa7-5c9e-b69d-33abee26fafa', 'word_count': 1116, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c9b9ff2f-7fa7-5c9e-b69d-33abee26fafa'}, {'abstract': 'Video and photos taken by a Times reporter, Anne Barnard, show both scenes of destruction and a seemingly normal city: people just continuing on with their daily lives.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/09/world/middleeast/syria-aleppo-war.html', 'snippet': 'Video and photos taken by a Times reporter, Anne Barnard, show both scenes of destruction and a seemingly normal city: people just continuing on with their daily lives.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I took this video while on what often felt like a surreal bus ride through war-torn Syria. The video begins on the edge of the city of Aleppo, its western, government-held half. The area we drove through is all government held but has been intensely fought over, again and again, and has changed hands several times. The route we took connects the city to a key supply route — whoever controls the road determines if the eastern, rebel-held part of the city is besieged or not.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'My Journey Into Aleppo: Watching a Moonscape of War Turn Into a Functioning City', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Journey Into Aleppo: Amid Moonscape of War, a Functioning City', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'vis-dispatch', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ALEPPO (SYRIA)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-08T10:00:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anne Barnard', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnard', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/95f7e28e-8082-5b4e-9067-fa269b3748d1', 'word_count': 1031, 'uri': 'nyt://article/95f7e28e-8082-5b4e-9067-fa269b3748d1'}, {'abstract': 'A recent conference of Russian exiles was perhaps a preview of what Russia will look like when it decides what to be next.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/09/opinion/what-russia-after-putin.html', 'snippet': 'A recent conference of Russian exiles was perhaps a preview of what Russia will look like when it decides what to be next.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Vilnius, Lithuania — If only because nothing lasts forever, because no one lives forever, the Putin era in Russia will eventually end. President Vladimir V. Putin is 64 years old, and those who oppose his regime are focusing their attention on imagining what will happen after him — especially since his government’s ongoing crackdown makes it so difficult to focus on the present.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/09/opinion/09Gessen-inyt/09Gessen-inyt-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 417, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/09/opinion/09Gessen-inyt/09Gessen-inyt-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 417}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/09/opinion/09Gessen-inyt/09Gessen-inyt-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 712, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/09/opinion/09Gessen-inyt/09Gessen-inyt-superJumbo.jpg', 'heig