erial': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e6ea6640-a257-5603-a665-5ac6d06d59a9', 'word_count': 994, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e6ea6640-a257-5603-a665-5ac6d06d59a9'}, {'abstract': 'The pianist Barry Harris; the drummer Jimmy Cobb; the trumpeter Jimmy Owens; and the saxophonists George Coleman and Jimmy Heath will play Flushing Town Hall.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/13/arts/music/the-old-jazz-masters-they-keep-getting-better.html', 'snippet': 'The pianist Barry Harris; the drummer Jimmy Cobb; the trumpeter Jimmy Owens; and the saxophonists George Coleman and Jimmy Heath will play Flushing Town Hall.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Great jazz musicians don’t age out of their art. In many cases they just get better, trading impetuousness and flash for a deeper, more judicious, more emotionally resonant style. This has long been a reason to applaud the National Endowment for the Arts Jazz Masters program, a lifetime achievement award presented to a handful of artists every year since 1982. A concert at Flushing Town Hall on Friday, Nov. 18, will feature five such jazz masters: the pianist Barry Harris; the drummer Jimmy Cobb; the trumpeter Jimmy Owens; and the saxophonists George Coleman and Jimmy Heath. Three are in their 80s — Mr. Heath recently turned 90, and Mr. Owens will be 73 in a few weeks — and all have remained active in the clubs, in the studio and in the classroom. They’ll perform in an all-star group with one younger outlier, the bassist David Wong. Not counting him, there will be well over 300 years of professional experience onstage. (8 p.m., 137-35 Northern Boulevard, Queens, 718-463-7700 x222, flushingtownhall.org.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Old (Jazz) Masters, They Keep Getting Better', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Music; The Old Masters, Getting Better', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jazz', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Endowment for the Arts', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Flushing (Queens, NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-09T19:05:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Chinen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chinen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9f6b149b-de48-5d07-9aeb-b6c96d78c027', 'word_count': 170, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9f6b149b-de48-5d07-9aeb-b6c96d78c027'}, {'abstract': '“Southern Accent: Seeking the American South Contemporary Art,” at the Nasher Museum of Art at Duke University, includes 60 artists.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/13/arts/design/forget-the-coasts-look-to-the-hinterlands.html', 'snippet': '“Southern Accent: Seeking the American South Contemporary Art,” at the Nasher Museum of Art at Duke University, includes 60 artists.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When people think about contemporary American art, they tend to think only in coastal terms: New York and Los Angeles. Big mistake. There are alternative histories being written through the huge amounts of work going on elsewhere in this country. “Southern Accent: Seeking the American South in Contemporary Art,” at the Nasher Museum of Art at Duke University in Durham, N.C., gives a rich and complex sense of this.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/13/arts/13GUIDE7/13GUIDE7-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/13/arts/13GUIDE7/13GUIDE7-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/13/arts/13GUIDE7/13GUIDE7-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/13/arts/13GUIDE7/13GUIDE7-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 716, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/13/arts/13GUIDE7/13GUIDE7-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 716}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/13/arts/13GUIDE7/13GUIDE7-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 859, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/13/arts/13GUIDE7/13GUIDE7-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1718, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Forget the Coasts. Look to the Hinterlands.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Art; Looking Inland, and Inward', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nasher Museum of Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Durham (NC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Southern Accent: Seeking the American South in Contemporary Art (Exhibit)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-09T19:10:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Holland Cotter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Holland', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cotter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8b3dc0f1-73ff-541f-9bac-85e32f07db54', 'word_count': 162, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8b3dc0f1-73ff-541f-9bac-85e32f07db54'}, {'abstract': 'William Kentridge’s staging of the opera will include puppetry.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/13/arts/music/monteverdis-ulysses-at-the-white-light-festival.html', 'snippet': 'William Kentridge’s staging of the opera will include puppetry.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In his brilliant productions for the Metropolitan Opera of Shostakovich’s “The Nose” and Berg’s “Lulu,” the South African artist William Kentridge filled the house’s enormous stage with animations, activity and dazzling sets. Mr. Kentridge will reveal another, smaller, dimension to his artistry in his production of Monteverdi’s opera “The Return of Ulysses” for Lincoln Center’s White Light Festival. His staging of the opera, based on Homer’s tale, will use puppetry from Handspring Puppet Company posed before animated images. Monteverdi’s great score will be performed by a cast of singers and the Ricercar Consort, under Philippe Pierlot. (7:30 p.m., Monday, Nov. 14, through Wednesday, Gerald W. Lynch Theater at John Jay College, 212-721-6500; whitelightfestival.org)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/13/arts/13GUIDE1/13GUIDE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/13/arts/13GUIDE1/13GUIDE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/13/arts/13GUIDE1/13GUIDE1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/13/arts/13GUIDE1/13GUIDE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1001, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/13/arts/13GUIDE1/13GUIDE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1001}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/13/arts/13GUIDE1/13GUIDE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 614, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/13/arts/13GUIDE1/13GUIDE1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1228, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Monteverdi’s ‘Ulysses’ at the White Light Festival', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Classical; Ulysses Returns, With Puppets', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kentridge, William', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Monteverdi, Claudio', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Return of Ulysses  (Opera)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-09T19:14:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/78ea6ea3-7a37-58dd-92ed-e2dd37d616a3', 'word_count': 170, 'uri': 'nyt://article/78ea6ea3-7a37-58dd-92ed-e2dd37d616a3'}, {'abstract': 'The people behind the sounds of Elvis Presley, the Beatles, Johnny Cash and more are part of this series exploring the recording process.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/13/arts/television/going-inside-the-studio-in-soundbreaking-on-pbs.html', 'snippet': 'The people behind the sounds of Elvis Presley, the Beatles, Johnny Cash and more are part of this series exploring the recording process.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The performer is often only the public face of a well-known song; its body is formed in the studio. That part of the creative process is explored in the richly detailed PBS series “Soundbreaking: Stories From the Cutting Edge of Recorded Music,” an eight-parter that begins Monday, Nov. 14.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/13/arts/13GUIDE4/13GUIDE4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/13/arts/13GUIDE4/13GUIDE4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/13/arts/13GUIDE4/13GUIDE4-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/13/arts/13GUIDE4/13GUIDE4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 807, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/13/arts/13GUIDE4/13GUIDE4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 807}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/13/arts/13GUIDE4/13GUIDE4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 762, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/13/arts/13GUIDE4/13GUIDE4-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1862, 'width': 1385, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Going Inside the Studio in ‘Soundbreaking’ on PBS', 'kicker': 'Television', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Back Story: Elvis and More', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Public Broadcasting Service', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Soundbreaking: Stories From the Cutting Edge of Recorded Music (TV Program)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-09T19:15:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e9aa0487-593e-57c7-9fca-dc18d51ac36e', 'word_count': 166, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e9aa0487-593e-57c7-9fca-dc18d51ac36e'}, {'abstract': 'States that lost the most incoming freshmen to public universities elsewhere, and states that gained the most.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/06/education/edlife/out-of-state-students-winners-and-losers.html', 'snippet': 'States that lost the most incoming freshmen to public universities elsewhere, and states that gained the most.', 'lead_paragraph': 'States that lost the most incoming freshmen to public universities elsewhere, and states that gained the most. Data is from the Department of Education for 2014.', 'print_section': 'ED', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Out-of-State Students: Winners and Losers', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Great Student Migration', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Karr, Jane', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'States (US)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-09T19:15:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'EdLife', 'section_name': 'Education', 'subsection_name': 'Education Life', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jane Karr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Karr', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/78af87d2-386f-54e7-b7a5-213bb20872a5', 'word_count': 590, 'uri': 'nyt://article/78af87d2-386f-54e7-b7a5-213bb20872a5'}, {'abstract': 'The troupe moves to sounds as varied as Bach and Jeff Buckley at New York city Center.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/13/arts/dance/smooth-complex-potent-nederlands-dans-theater.html', 'snippet': 'The troupe moves to sounds as varied as Bach and Jeff Buckley at New York city Center.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If the world’s dance companies occupied the shelves of a bar, Nederlands Dans Theater would sit comfortably on the top like a fine liquor — smooth, complex, potent. In 2011, Paul Lightfoot became the artistic director after serving as the company’s house choreographer, along with his creative (and romantic) partner Sol León, since 2002. On this visit, the company presents their works “Stop-Motion,” where dancers slide through white dust like ghosts on a ski slope, and “Safe as Houses,” where they are chased by a massive rotating wall to the sounds of Bach. Also on the program are new creations by the sharp associate choreographers Crystal Pite and Marco Goecke. She sets the action on a long table and clads her dancers in suits and ties, like a corporate thriller; he interprets the acoustic love songs of Jeff Buckley. On Tuesday, Nov. 15, the company will offer a preview as part of the Guggenheim’s Works and Process series. (Wednesday through Saturday, New York City Center; nycitycenter.org)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/13/arts/13GUIDE5/13GUIDE5-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 397, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/13/arts/13GUIDE5/13GUIDE5-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 397}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/13/arts/13GUIDE5/13GUIDE5-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 677, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/13/arts/13GUIDE5/13GUIDE5-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1354, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/13/arts/13GUIDE5/13GUIDE5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/13/arts/13GUIDE5/13GUIDE5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/13/arts/13GUIDE5/13GUIDE5-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Smooth, Complex, Potent: Nederlands Dans Theater', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dance; Potent, Smooth and Complex', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nederlands Dans Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York City Center Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-09T19:17:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Schaefer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schaefer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f222a4b0-127c-57f1-9c45-af2c0ae3e19e', 'word_count': 166, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f222a4b0-127c-57f1-9c45-af2c0ae3e19e'}, {'abstract': 'A tribute to Mr. Hanks at the Museum of Modern Art includes a screening of his 1984 comedy “Bachelor Party.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/13/movies/early-tom-hanks-headed-for-a-raucous-party.html', 'snippet': 'A tribute to Mr. Hanks at the Museum of Modern Art includes a screening of his 1984 comedy “Bachelor Party.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tom Hanks has been getting plenty of work lately, in films like “Sully” and “Inferno” and as part of a notorious “Saturday Night Live” election skit. The Museum of Modern Art remembers a time when he was still on his way to becoming a household name when it shows “Bachelor Party,” his 1984 comedy, on Sunday, Nov. 13, as part of “Tom Hanks: A Tribute.” Mr. Hanks played half of a couple headed toward the altar (Tawny Kitaen played his fiancée) when the event of the film’s title disrupts things. “Mr. Hanks’s suave smart-alecky manner owes a lot to Bill Murray, but he’s funny and engaging even if he isn’t doing anything new,” Janet Maslin wrote in her review for The New York Times. The film shows at 5:30 p.m. At 2:30 that same afternoon, “Big” (1988) will be screened; on Monday, at 4 p.m., comes “A League of Their Own” (1992). (Roy and Niuta Titus Theaters; moma.org.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/13/arts/13GUIDE2/13GUIDE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 563, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/13/arts/13GUIDE2/13GUIDE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 563}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/13/arts/13GUIDE2/13GUIDE2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 961, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/13/arts/13GUIDE2/13GUIDE2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1922, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/13/arts/13GUIDE2/13GUIDE2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/13/arts/13GUIDE2/13GUIDE2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/13/arts/13GUIDE2/13GUIDE2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Early Tom Hanks, Headed for a Raucous Party', 'kicker': 'Film', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Tribute to Tom Hanks', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hanks, Tom', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Bachelor Party (Movie)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Museum of Modern Art', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'BiG (Movie)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'A League of Their Own (Movie)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-09T19:18:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a07acc6f-9777-51b5-b23f-08d2334f98e5', 'word_count': 158, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a07acc6f-9777-51b5-b23f-08d2334f98e5'}, {'abstract': 'An early work by Ms. Parks, “The Death of the Last Black Man in the Whole Entire World, a.k.a. The Negro Book of the Dead,” opens Signature’s season.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/13/theater/suzan-lori-parks-defying-genres.html', 'snippet': 'An early work by Ms. Parks, “The Death of the Last Black Man in the Whole Entire World, a.k.a. The Negro Book of the Dead,” opens Signature’s season.', 'lead_paragraph': 'How do you portray an abyss that has swallowed up a cultural past? More urgently, how do you fill it? Knotty questions abound in Suzan-Lori Parks’s “The Death of the Last Black Man in the Whole Entire World, a.k.a. The Negro Book of the Dead,” which opens on Sunday, Nov. 13. The script for this genre-defying work from 1990 specifies that the set include “an exact replica of the Great Hole in History,” into which entire chapters of the African-American experience have disappeared.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_pag