 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Graham Bowley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Graham', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bowley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/39288769-bbc4-5e28-b392-fa0e28b74c7d', 'word_count': 406, 'uri': 'nyt://article/39288769-bbc4-5e28-b392-fa0e28b74c7d'}, {'abstract': 'In Michael Connelly’s latest Harry Bosch book, his investigator is on the hunt for a serial rapist while also searching for a billionaire’s heir.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/10/books/review-michael-connelly-wrong-side-of-goodbye.html', 'snippet': 'In Michael Connelly’s latest Harry Bosch book, his investigator is on the hunt for a serial rapist while also searching for a billionaire’s heir.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Each of Michael Connelly’s Harry Bosch books has a way of referring to earlier ones in the series, as when his latest, “The Wrong Side of Goodbye,” brings up something about a plastic surgeon. That surgeon figured in “The Crossing,” one of the series’s better recent installments. And it came out only a year ago. Still, I had to look it up, because the characters aren’t what make Mr. Connelly’s books worthwhile. The classic mystery plotting and streamlined storytelling are what render him so readable. Of all the big-name writers who dominate this genre, Mr. Connelly is the most solid, old-school pro.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/10/arts/10BOOKCONNELLY2/10BOOKCONNELLY2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/10/arts/10BOOKCONNELLY2/10BOOKCONNELLY2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/10/arts/10BOOKCONNELLY2/10BOOKCONNELLY2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/10/arts/10BOOKCONNELLY2/10BOOKCONNELLY2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 946, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/10/arts/10BOOKCONNELLY2/10BOOKCONNELLY2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 946}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/10/arts/10BOOKCONNELLY2/10BOOKCONNELLY2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 650, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/10/arts/10BOOKCONNELLY2/10BOOKCONNELLY2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1200, 'width': 761, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: Michael Connelly’s ‘The Wrong Side of Goodbye,’ a Mystery Traveling the Freeways', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Dogged Investigator Searches for a Rapist and a Billionaire’s Heir', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Connelly, Michael (1956- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Wrong Side of Goodbye (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-09T21:52:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Janet Maslin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Janet', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Maslin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/44ac5c1a-a609-5b04-8941-7749ea410868', 'word_count': 685, 'uri': 'nyt://article/44ac5c1a-a609-5b04-8941-7749ea410868'}, {'abstract': 'Researchers tracked narwhals and found that they reconstruct their underwater world with more resolution that most other animals on the planet.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/10/science/narwhals-echolocation.html', 'snippet': 'Researchers tracked narwhals and found that they reconstruct their underwater world with more resolution that most other animals on the planet.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The narwhal is not an aquatic unicorn. It’s not magical, or mythical. It’s just a whale with two teeth, one of which happens to be really long on males. But it’s not just its snaggletooth — which can be up to nine feet long — that makes this Arctic sea creature unbelievable. The narwhal sees with sound — and it’s exceptionally good at it too, according to a study published Wednesday in the journal PLOS One.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/10/science/09TB-Narwhal/09TB-Narwhal-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/10/science/09TB-Narwhal/09TB-Narwhal-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/10/science/09TB-Narwhal/09TB-Narwhal-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/10/science/09TB-Narwhal/09TB-Narwhal-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/10/science/09TB-Narwhal/09TB-Narwhal-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/10/science/09TB-Narwhal/09TB-Narwhal-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/10/science/09TB-Narwhal/09TB-Narwhal-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Narwhals, Tusked Whales of the Arctic, See With Sound. Really Well.', 'kicker': 'Trilobites', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'SoundScapes: Narwhals Click Away to Envision Their World', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Whales and Whaling', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sonar', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ARCTIC REGIONS', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Canada', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Public Library of Science (PLoS)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Laidre, Kristin L', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'PLoS One (Journal)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACOUSTICS', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-09T21:53:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joanna Klein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joanna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Klein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1f31f6e2-ee14-5504-980d-4255d796cc8c', 'word_count': 634, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1f31f6e2-ee14-5504-980d-4255d796cc8c'}, {'abstract': 'After writing four vampire novels, Ms. Meyer seems to have grown allergic to repeating herself. “I get a little bored,” she said in an interview.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/10/books/sorry-twilight-fans-stephenie-meyers-latest-is-a-twisted-spy-thriller.html', 'snippet': 'After writing four vampire novels, Ms. Meyer seems to have grown allergic to repeating herself. “I get a little bored,” she said in an interview.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Stephenie Meyer’s millions of fans have come to expect certain supernatural flourishes from her novels, which feature shape-shifters, vampires and werewolves, even vampire-werewolf hybrids. (Her 2008 novel, “The Host,” centered on parasitic aliens that occupy human bodies, and even that was too blandly terrestrial for some readers.)', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/09/arts/10MEYER/10MEYER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/09/arts/10MEYER/10MEYER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/09/arts/10MEYER/10MEYER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/09/arts/10MEYER/10MEYER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/09/arts/10MEYER/10MEYER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 