s', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mary Billard', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Billard', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d5cf7037-5385-5a1e-bf3d-89f714d78016', 'word_count': 1334, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d5cf7037-5385-5a1e-bf3d-89f714d78016'}, {'abstract': 'Most of us will never take these trips. But I feel improved for having read about them.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/10/t-magazine/ts-new-travel-issue-editors-letter.html', 'snippet': 'Most of us will never take these trips. But I feel improved for having read about them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In her “Sign of the Times” essay, Sadie Stein writes about how we rarely take the kind of holidays many of our parents enjoyed — to a resort where we are coddled like children and the most taxing activity is deciding between reading and napping. Instead, now we strive for self-improvement at every opportunity, even in our down time. We cleanse, do yoga, climb mountains and so on.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/13/t-magazine/13tmag-12edletter-t_CA0/13tmag-12edletter-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 386, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/13/t-magazine/13tmag-12edletter-t_CA0/13tmag-12edletter-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 386}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/13/t-magazine/13tmag-12edletter-t_CA0/13tmag-12edletter-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 659, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': '