d its deeply embedded histories of injustice, poverty and loss.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/11/arts/11BUCHANANJP1/11BUCHANANJP1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/11/arts/11BUCHANANJP1/11BUCHANANJP1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/11/arts/11BUCHANANJP1/11BUCHANANJP1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/11/arts/11BUCHANANJP1/11BUCHANANJP1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/11/arts/11BUCHANANJP1/11BUCHANANJP1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/11/arts/11BUCHANANJP1/11BUCHANANJP1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/11/arts/11BUCHANANJP1/11BUCHANANJP1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Haunting ‘Ruins and Rituals’ Begins ‘A Year of Yes’ at Brooklyn Museum', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Architecture of Survival', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rosenberg, Karen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Monuments and Memorials (Structures)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Museum', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Buchanan, Beverly (1940-2015)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-10T22:27:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Rosenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/734435ab-d3d6-58c6-9fd1-827c485e96e3', 'word_count': 999, 'uri': 'nyt://article/734435ab-d3d6-58c6-9fd1-827c485e96e3'}, {'abstract': 'Zoe Kazan plays a mother driving into the night with her daughter (Ella Ballentine) in this chiller written and directed by Bryan Bertino.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/11/movies/the-monster-review.html', 'snippet': 'Zoe Kazan plays a mother driving into the night with her daughter (Ella Ballentine) in this chiller written and directed by Bryan Bertino.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Aliens may attack while serial killers rev their chain saws, but few monsters seem as reliably scary as mommy dearest. Whether she’s slumped (and dead) in her rocking chair, as in “Psycho,” or pulling a knife on her daughter, as in “Carrie,” there’s just something about mothers that brings out the worst and sometimes the best shivers. For Freud, Medusa’s head, with its writhing snakes, represents the mother’s genitals. The terror of Medusa is the terror of castration, which occurs when a boy sees that his mother doesn’t have a penis — voilà, anxiety!', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedi