 gentlemen,” Mark Twain said in a public lecture at the time, “I hear you, & so know that you are here — & I am here, too, notwithstanding I am not visible.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/11/science/11AIRPOLLUTION1/11AIRPOLLUTION1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/11/science/11AIRPOLLUTION1/11AIRPOLLUTION1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/11/science/11AIRPOLLUTION1/11AIRPOLLUTION1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/11/science/11AIRPOLLUTION1/11AIRPOLLUTION1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 438, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/11/science/11AIRPOLLUTION1/11AIRPOLLUTION1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 438}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/11/science/11AIRPOLLUTION1/11AIRPOLLUTION1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 747, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/11/science/11AIRPOLLUTION1/11AIRPOLLUTION1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1494, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Lesson for India in a Fog So Thick It Could Kill a Cow', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lessons From a London Fog So Thick It Could Kill a Cow', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Air Pollution', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tavernise, Sabrina', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Third World and Developing Countries', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-10T22:37:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sabrina Tavernise', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sabrina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tavernise', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/86af2180-3ae3-5686-9a97-c373ecd6178f', 'word_count': 879, 'uri': 'nyt://article/86af2180-3ae3-5686-9a97-c373ecd6178f'}, {'abstract': 'Paul Verhoeven’s film is a psychological thriller, a strangely dry-eyed melodrama, a kinky sex farce and a savage comedy of bourgeois manners.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/11/movies/elle-review-isabelle-huppert.html', 'snippet': 'Paul Verhoeven’s film is a psychological thriller, a strangely dry-eyed melodrama, a kinky sex farce and a savage comedy of bourgeois manners.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The opening scene of “Elle” is a shocker: a brutal sexual assault witnessed by a house cat and filmed with pitiless detachment. “The opposite of a trigger warning,” as a friend of mine said. Everything that follows is, in some ways, even more shocking, as the movie — a masterpiece of suave perversity, directed by Paul Verhoeven — leads its audience through a meticulously constructed maze of ambiguity, scrambling our assumptions and expectations at every turn, dispensing discomfort and delight and daring us to distinguish one from the other.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/11/arts/11ELLE/11ELLE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 369, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/11/arts/11ELLE/11ELLE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 369}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/11/arts/11ELLE/11ELLE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 630, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/11/arts/11ELLE/11ELLE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1260, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/11/arts/11ELLE/11ELLE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/11/arts/11ELLE/11ELLE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/11/arts/11ELLE/11ELLE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: In ‘Elle,’ Isabelle Huppert Subverts the Role of Rape Victim', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Kinkily Turning the Tables on a Rapist', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Elle (Movie)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Huppert, Isabelle', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Verhoeven, Paul', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-10T22:37:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/10da129f-88a5-54b8-8397-02b4aecd2d71', 'word_count': 966, 'uri': 'nyt://article/10da129f-88a5-54b8-8397-02b4aecd2d71'}, {'abstract': 'In Denis Villeneuve’s film, Amy Adams plays a linguist struggling to understand interplanetary visitors checking in at 12 spots around the globe.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/11/movies/arrival-review-amy-adams-jeremy-renner.html', 'snippet': 'In Denis Villeneuve’s film, Amy Adams plays a linguist struggling to understand interplanetary visitors checking in at 12 spots around the globe.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In what’s perhaps the first science-fiction film — Georges Méliès’s 1902 marvel “A Trip to the Moon” — a group of astronomers lands in the Man in the Moon’s eye. They’re soon taken prisoner by moon men, but a quick-witted astronomer clobbers the king and they escape, with one earthling vanquishing moon men with an umbrella. Humans have been zapping extraterrestrials ever since. It’s so easy to fight the unknown, at least in movies.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/11/arts/11ARRIVAL/11ARRIVAL-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/11/arts/11ARRIVAL/11ARRIVAL-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'captio