rities for the New President', 'kicker': 'Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '3 Tasks for the New No. 1', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2016', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-12T19:30:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/f2cbeebd-17dc-59c2-a9e1-46366f22a81f', 'word_count': 921, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f2cbeebd-17dc-59c2-a9e1-46366f22a81f'}, {'abstract': 'An extraordinary period of globalization has ended. The period from World War I through World War II offers lessons for what might come next.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/13/opinion/sunday/when-borders-close.html', 'snippet': 'An extraordinary period of globalization has ended. The period from World War I through World War II offers lessons for what might come next.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The age of globalization generated great prosperity. As the flow of goods, money and people across borders surged, millions benefited. But the elite gained the most. And as inequality rose, it stirred pockets of fierce resentment among those left behind. When the great shock came, the discontented turned to nationalist firebrands, who promised to impose controls on free trade, global banks and immigrants. Globalization stalled. A new age of deglobalization hit full stride.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/13/opinion/sunday/13sharma/13sharma-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 217, 'width'