omy, but it has been struggling to lift a lackluster growth rate and escape the trap of deflation — sagging consumer prices associated with weak output and declining living standards. So what went right last quarter? And will it last?', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/15/world/15JAPANECON-1/15JAPANECON-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 410, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/15/world/15JAPANECON-1/15JAPANECON-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 410}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/15/world/15JAPANECON-1/15JAPANECON-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 700, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/15/world/15JAPANECON-1/15JAPANECON-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1400, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/15/world/15JAPANECON-1/15JAPANECON-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/15/world/15JAPANECON-1/15JAPANECON-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/15/world/15JAPANECON-1/15JAPANECON-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Japan’s Economy Posted Surprisingly Strong Growth', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Why Japan’s Economy Posted Strong Growth in Third Quarter', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Soble, Jonathan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Trade and World Market', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ABE, SHINZO', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-14T02:44:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Soble', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Soble', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/623c24bb-f06d-5107-b072-a0cddea96ac0', 'word_count': 499, 'uri': 'nyt://article/623c24bb-f06d-5107-b072-a0cddea96ac0'}, {'abstract': 'Suzan-Lori Parks’s phantasmagorical theater piece is a sepulchral parade of images that have distorted and swallowed up the history of African-Americans.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/14/theater/death-of-the-last-black-man-review.html', 'snippet': 'Suzan-Lori Parks’s phantasmagorical theater piece is a sepulchral parade of images that have distorted and swallowed up the history of African-Americans.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Surely no fruit has ever been heavier than the watermelon now on display at the Pershing Square Signature Center. Make that watermelons, plural, though for all intents and purposes (and those are myriad), they are one oppressive entity.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/14/arts/14DEATHBLACK/14DEATHBLACK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/14/arts/14DEATHBLACK/14DEATHBLACK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/14/arts/14DEATHBLACK/14DEATHBLACK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/14/arts/14DEATHBLACK/14DEATHBLACK-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1369, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/14/arts/14DEATHBLACK/14DEATHBLACK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/14/arts/14DEATHBLACK/14DEATHBLACK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/14/arts/14DEATHBLACK/14DEATHBLACK-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: ‘The Death of the Last Black Man,’ a Nightmare Hypnotically Retold', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Historical Nightmare of Racism, Hypnotically Retold', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Parks, Suzan-Lori', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Blain-Cruz, Lileana', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Signature Center', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Death of the Last Black Man in the Whole Entire World AKA the Negro Book of the Dead (Play)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-14T03:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Brantley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brantley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3639230d-28e9-5098-bdfa-b48c0704560b', 'word_count': 892, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3639230d-28e9-5098-bdfa-b48c0704560b'}, {'abstract': 'This one-man show revolves around a man who has apparently simply dialed a wrong number and then stays on the line to talk. And talk.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/14/theater/daniel-kitson-mouse-review.html', 'snippet': 'This one-man show revolves around a man who has apparently simply dialed a wrong number and then stays on the line to talk. And talk.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Try tracking all the conversations going on in Daniel Kitson’s “Mouse: The Persistence of an Unlikely Thought,” which opened on Sunday at St. Ann’s Warehouse in Brooklyn, and your mind will probably short-circuit. There is, to start with the obvious, the dialogue between the title creature (of unknown gender) of this solo performance piece and the woman (of unspecified name) who discovers it on her staircase.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: Daniel Kitson’s ‘Mouse’ Tells Its Tales Over a Speakerphone', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sorry, Wrong Number, but Hey, Let’s Have a Talk', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Mouse: The Persistence of an Unlikely Thought (Play)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kitson, Daniel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "St Ann's Warehouse (Brooklyn, NY)", 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-14T03:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Brantley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brantley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/988431f5-fa8f-5540-b2a5-d064d37ddebe', 'word_count': 612, 'uri': 'nyt://article/988431f5-fa8f-5540-b2a5-d064d37ddebe'}, {'abstract': 'The truth regarding a popular fan theory comes to light, as do the lengths Dr. Ford will travel to protect his vision.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/13/arts/television/westworld-season-1-episode-7-recap-bernard.html', 'snippet': 'The truth regarding a popular fan theory comes to light, as do the lengths Dr. Ford will travel to protect his vision.', 'lead_paragraph': 'And so a popular fan theory comes to fruition: One of the park’s behind-the-scenes engineers is an android, and that android is Bernard, the head of Westworld’s programming division. Bernard won’t likely be the last, but he was always the most obvious, given his close association with Ford, his surreptitious exchanges with Dolores, and the circumstances surrounding his son’s death, which have been shrouded in vagueness, like a half-completed memory. Jeffrey Wright specializes in playing intense, introspective characters, but the slight lack of affect he’s brought to Bernard has long hinted at the synthetic hardware within. The story about his son, like Maeve’s memories of her daughter, has given Bernard the dimensionality of a human, but not quite the authentic depth.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/13/watching/13westworld/13westworld-articleLarge.jp