name': 'Egner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7155560b-9a30-5f05-8e15-35aa49d7cd35', 'word_count': 1257, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7155560b-9a30-5f05-8e15-35aa49d7cd35'}, {'abstract': 'The host Stephen J. Dubner and panelists consider mules, their defenders and other wonders of the natural world, including marine animals that will either live forever or kill you — or both.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/12/podcasts/stephen-dubner-tell-me-something-i-dont-know-mule-lobby.html', 'snippet': 'The host Stephen J. Dubner and panelists consider mules, their defenders and other wonders of the natural world, including marine animals that will either live forever or kill you — or both.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1885, a 5-year-old Douglas MacArthur spotted a certain African animal roaming across the American Southwest. That’s because 50 years earlier, Army officers wanted to test out the animal as an upgrade to the typical mules and horses they used as pack animals. The replacements worked out extraordinarily well — but then the Civil War intervened, and the program was abandoned. (Plus, the mule lobby wasn’t too thrilled about these outsider animals coming to take over what they argued the mules were doing perfectly well.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/12/podcasts/12TMSIDK-image/12TMSIDK-image-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/12/podcasts/12TMSIDK-image/12TMSIDK-image-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/12/podcasts/12TMSIDK-image/12TMSIDK-image-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height':