 The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/27e19fdf-aeaf-5148-a54b-6c643d8eef82', 'word_count': 555, 'uri': 'nyt://article/27e19fdf-aeaf-5148-a54b-6c643d8eef82'}, {'abstract': 'Issa and Molly deal with the aftermath of their own mistakes in starkly different ways.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/13/arts/television/insecure-season-1-episode-6-recap.html', 'snippet': 'Issa and Molly deal with the aftermath of their own mistakes in starkly different ways.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A powerful, even overwhelming, feeling comes with recognizing yourself in a TV show. As the medium grows more diverse, there is greater opportunity for this. Recently, I’ve found myself in various conversations about whether the lead characters of “Insecure” are “relatable.” As a black woman, I actually don’t relate to Issa and Molly at all. We come from drastically different cultural backgrounds, our goals take on different shapes and our romantic pitfalls exist on opposing wavelengths.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/13/watching/13insecure/13insecure-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/13/watching/13insecure/13insecure-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/13/watching/13insecure/13insecure-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/13/watching/13insecure/13insecure-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1362, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'sub