d (Mass)", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Massachusetts', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-14T19:22:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Benjamin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Benjamin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/078e7298-4666-50e8-9393-cfe151e03e75', 'word_count': 1833, 'uri': 'nyt://article/078e7298-4666-50e8-9393-cfe151e03e75'}, {'abstract': 'The “advice of counsel” defense is raised with increased regularity in white-collar crime cases, often coupled with its close cousin – acting in good faith.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/15/business/dealbook/martin-shkreli-may-point-the-finger-at-his-lawyers.html', 'snippet': 'The “advice of counsel” defense is raised with increased regularity in white-collar crime cases, often coupled with its close cousin – acting in good faith.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lawyers are ubiquitous in the business world, advising at every stage, from the formation of a company through its operating stage to its eventual demise, whether by acquisition or perhaps dissolution in bankruptcy. When corporate executives get in trouble, it is common for them to claim that their lawyers said it was permissible to act that way.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Martin Shkreli May Point the Finger at His Lawyers', 'kicker': 'White Collar Watch', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Securities and Commodities Violations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Retrophin Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'v