ers as his “posse” in an interview published by ESPN on Monday drew an angry response from James, who took offense at the racial connotation of the word.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/16/sports/16JACKSONWEB3/16JACKSONWEB3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/16/sports/16JACKSONWEB3/16JACKSONWEB3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/16/sports/16JACKSONWEB3/16JACKSONWEB3-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/16/sports/16JACKSONWEB3/16JACKSONWEB3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/16/sports/16JACKSONWEB3/16JACKSONWEB3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/16/sports/16JACKSONWEB3/16JACKSONWEB3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/16/sports/16JACKSONWEB3/16JACKSONWEB3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'LeBron James Fires Back at Phil Jackson for ‘Posse’ Comment', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Angered by ‘Posse’ Remark, James Quickly Fires Back', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'James, LeBron', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jackson, Phil (1945- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ANTHONY, CARMELO', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Knicks', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carter, Maverick', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-15T20:43:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Vorkunov', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vorkunov', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ceb52469-8bb6-54c2-b666-d42417ecb63c', 'word_count': 887, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ceb52469-8bb6-54c2-b666-d42417ecb63c'}, {'abstract': 'A new exhibition combines works by Willem de Kooning and Zao Wou-Ki.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/15/t-magazine/art/willem-de-kooning-zao-wou-ki-two-men-show.html', 'snippet': 'A new exhibition combines works by Willem de Kooning and Zao Wou-Ki.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To coincide with the expansion of her Madison Avenue gallery next month, Dominique Lévy is mounting an exhibition of side-by-side works by Willem de Kooning and Zao Wou-Ki (currently the subject of a solo show at the Asia Society). The two very different expressive painters were both restless experimenters. Frustrated with the formal limitations of the Chinese academy, Zao left for Paris in 1948, while de Kooning, born in the Netherlands and also classically trained, arrived in New York in 1927. The artists found their signature styles around the same time: de Kooning’s infamous “Woman” canvases, exhibited in 1953, broke his association with Abstract Expressionism, and his aggressive dismantling of the female body still unsettles. A year later, Zao changed brushes and technique to create his signature chaotic landscapes. His swirling “4-6-62” has the feel of a tornado, or an atom bomb. Lévy describes the show as “a combat between two giants.’’', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '68', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/16/t-magazine/art-exhibit-slide-QQXB/art-exhibit-slide-QQXB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 346, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/16/t-magazine/art-exhibit-slide-QQXB/art-exhibit-slide-QQXB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 346}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/16/t-magazine/art-exhibit-slide-QQXB/art-exhibit-slide-QQXB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 590, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/16/t-magazine/art-exhibit-slide-QQXB/art-exhibit-slide-QQXB-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 864, 'width': 1500, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/16/t-magazine/art-exhibit-slide-QQXB/art-exhibit-slide-QQXB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/16/t-magazine/art-exhibit-slide-QQXB/art-exhibit-slide-QQXB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailhe