ea, a literary historian, discusses the flowering of comedy, farce and satire in early 20th-century China, and the parallels to be found today.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/17/world/asia/china-humor-christopher-rea.html', 'snippet': 'Christopher Rea, a literary historian, discusses the flowering of comedy, farce and satire in early 20th-century China, and the parallels to be found today.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Christopher Rea is a literary and cultural historian at the University of British Columbia. A 39-year-old native of Berkeley, Calif., he has written on major figures in modern Chinese literature, including Qian Zhongshu and Yang Jiang. His new work, “The Age of Irreverence: A New History of Laughter in China,” shows how the early 20th century inspired a flowering of comedy, farce, cursing and satire. In it, one finds interesting parallels to today, especially in how people stick it to authority.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/17/world/17CHINAREA-3/17CHINAREA-3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/17/world/17CHINAREA-3/17CHINAREA-3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/17/world/17CHINAREA-3/17CHINAREA-3-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/17/world/17CHINAREA-3/17CHINAREA-3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/17/world/17CHINAREA-3/17CHINAREA-3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'r