 'uri': 'nyt://article/b4406bf6-a0c2-581c-a039-67a223251ccb'}, {'abstract': 'There’s a certain gravity in Mitski’s indie-rock songs, whether stubbornly trudging ballads or distortion-laced punk.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/20/arts/music/mitskis-emotional-undertow.html', 'snippet': 'There’s a certain gravity in Mitski’s indie-rock songs, whether stubbornly trudging ballads or distortion-laced punk.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There’s a certain gravity in Mitski’s indie-rock songs, whether they’re stubbornly trudging ballads or distortion-laced punk. Part of it is that Mitski Miyawaki is her band’s bass player and singer onstage (as well as its songwriter and multi-instrumentalist in the studio); her bass lines shadow her own voice from below, adding a constant undertow to her graceful, long-breathed melodies. And part of it is that in her songs, weighty emotional burdens are being exposed, confronted and borne, somberly and patiently. “One morning this sadness will fossilize,” she croons in a song on “Puberty 2,” her latest album, “And I will forget how to cry.” (Monday, Nov. 21, at Webster Hall in Manhattan; Tuesday at Market Hotel in Brooklyn.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/20/arts/20GUIDE7/20GUIDE7-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/20/arts/20GUIDE7/20GUIDE7-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/20/arts/20GUIDE7/20GUIDE7-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/20/arts/20