, 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/15/t-magazine/art/15tmag-robert-longo-slide-LLEA/15tmag-robert-longo-slide-LLEA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 395, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/15/t-magazine/art/15tmag-robert-longo-slide-LLEA/15tmag-robert-longo-slide-LLEA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 395}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/15/t-magazine/art/15tmag-robert-longo-slide-LLEA/15tmag-robert-longo-slide-LLEA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 674, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/15/t-magazine/art/15tmag-robert-longo-slide-LLEA/15tmag-robert-longo-slide-LLEA-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1348, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Robert Longo, Refashioned', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2016-11-16T17:12:20+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'T Magazine', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'subsection_name': 'Art', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/a9f55292-8239-56f5-ad06-6478e1ed8a9d', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/a9f55292-8239-56f5-ad06-6478e1ed8a9d'}, {'abstract': 'A new show of work by Robert Longo pairs his recent, politically inspired drawings with slow-motion versions of the Russian master’s films.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/16/t-magazine/art/robert-longo-proof-moscow-garage.html', 'snippet': 'A new show of work by Robert Longo pairs his recent, politically inspired drawings with slow-motion versions of the Russian master’s films.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At 63, Robert Longo has become an increasingly topical artist. Often lumped in with the three other male commercial behemoths of his generation — Eric Fischl, David Salle and Julian Schnabel, or “The Four Brushmen of the Apocalypse,” as Lynn Hirschberg called them in a cheeky 1987 Esquire story — Longo has, in the last two years, refashioned his art in a more blatantly political image. He created a modern classic with his diptych, “Untitled (Ferguson Police, August 13, 2014),” a photorealist depiction of Ferguson, Mo. police officers in riot gear, partly shrouded in a cloud of tear gas, the golden arches of a McDonald’s restaurant visible in the background. (He’d follow this thread further in 2016 with a drawing of the Baltimore police during the riots over the death of Freddie Gray.) His “Untitled (Football Players, Rams, Hands Up),” 2016, featured the St. Louis football team’s receivers entering the stadium for a game and making the “hands up, don’t shoot” gesture that has become a familiar symbol in Black Lives Matter protests. Another charcoal drawing focuses on a close-up of a bullet hole in the window of the Charlie Hebdo offices in Paris.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/15/t-magazine/art/15tmag-robert-longo-slide-2XRO/15tmag-robert-longo-slide-2XRO-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 324, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/15/t-magazine/art/15tmag-robert-longo-slide-2XRO/15tmag-robert-longo-slide-2XRO-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 324}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/15/t-magazine/art/15tmag-robert-longo-slide-2XRO/15tmag-robert-longo-slide-2XRO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 553, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 