sk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter K. Enns and Jonathon P. Schuldt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': 'K.', 'lastname': 'Enns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Jonathon', 'middlename': 'P.', 'lastname': 'Schuldt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/87c43abb-b1e1-5a77-bf84-94255f021347', 'word_count': 708, 'uri': 'nyt://article/87c43abb-b1e1-5a77-bf84-94255f021347'}, {'abstract': 'The arrival of the former Goldman Sachs partner as executive chairman and C.E.O. means there will be three insiders on Evercore’s board.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/17/business/dealbook/evercores-good-problem-of-adding-john-weinberg-to-the-board.html', 'snippet': 'The arrival of the former Goldman Sachs partner as executive chairman and C.E.O. means there will be three insiders on Evercore’s board.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Evercore’s two chairmen are an enviable problem. Wooing former Goldman Sachs partner John Weinberg is a coup for the $2.9 billion advisory firm. Making him executive chairman, though, muddies governance and creates top-heavy management.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Evercore’s ‘Good’ Problem of Adding John Weinberg to the Board', 'kicker': 'Breakingviews', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Goldman Sachs Group Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ALTMAN, ROGER C', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weinberg, John S', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schlosstein, Ralph', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Evercore Partners Inc', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-16T20:53:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'DealBook', 'byline': {'original': 'By Antony Currie', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Antony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Currie', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b5c20159-fa34-5cab-b38a-c4aea5d0f1a9', 'word_count': 348, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b5c20159-fa34-5cab-b38a-c4aea5d0f1a9'}, {'abstract': 'The conductor Leon Botstein will lead a rare performance of Harold Shapero’s masterpiece with the American Symphony Orchestra at Carnegie Hall.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/17/arts/music/a-symphony-with-powerful-champions-but-often-overlooked.html', 'snippet': 'The conductor Leon Botstein will lead a rare performance of Harold Shapero’s masterpiece with the American Symphony Orchestra at Carnegie Hall.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The American composer Harold Shapero’s Symphony for Classical Orchestra had languished in obscurity for some 30 years when André Previn conducted it with the Los Angeles Philharmonic in 1986. Audiences and local critics cheered. Alan Rich proclaimed the 45-minute piece “the greatest American symphony.” Martin Bernheimer, in The Los Angeles Times, wrote that Shapero revealed himself a “superb craftsman,” an “artist totally in control of the grandiose variables at hand.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/17/arts/17SHAPERO/17SHAPERO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/17/arts/17SHAPERO/17SHAPERO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, '