 voice on the tape is that of the writer and theologian John Hull, who at the time of the recording had been totally blind for almost two years. After losing his sight in his mid-40s, Dr. Hull, a newlywed with a young family, had decided that blindness would destroy him if he didn’t learn to understand it. For three years he recorded his experiences of sight loss, documenting “a world beyond sight.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Notes on Blindness: Acoustic Space', 'kicker': 'Op-Docs', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blindness', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACOUSTICS', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hull, John M', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diaries', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Disabilities', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-17T08:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Arnaud Colinart, Amaury La Burthe, Peter Middleton and James Spinney', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Arnaud', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Colinart', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Amaury', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'La Burthe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Middleton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}, {'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Spinney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 4}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/6e14ded0-2a74-551c-a2a9-ccd07f52d4a2', 'word_count': 338, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6e14ded0-2a74-551c-a2a9-ccd07f52d4a2'}, {'abstract': 'Losing my sight in my 80s has been like entering a new country.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/17/opinion/sunday/feeling-my-way-into-blindness.html', 'snippet': 'Losing my sight in my 80s has been like entering a new country.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Blindness is enveloping. It’s beyond belief to step outside and see so little, just a milky haze. Indoors, a smothering dark. It means that you can’t shed a mood of loneliness with a brisk walk down the street because you might trip, fall and break something. Nor will you see a passing friend, the sight of whom could be as cheery as an actual conversation. Sights, like sounds, randomly evoke a surge of memories ordinarily inaccessible that lighten and brighten the day. “Who are you?” I may already have asked 10 people who have spoken to me. Their body language as well as their smiles are lost to me. Human nature is striped with ambiguities, and you need to see them, but like a prisoner, I am hooded.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/16/opinion/17disability/16disability-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/16/opinion/17disability/16disability-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/16/opinion/17disability/16disability-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/16/opinion/17disability/16disability-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarg