 'nyt://article/b7f785b8-7c24-57de-8c65-51645e2dc2fe', 'word_count': 433, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b7f785b8-7c24-57de-8c65-51645e2dc2fe'}, {'abstract': 'Even fans of chess might struggle to connect with the cursory approach to this documentary’s subject, the prodigy Magnus Carlsen, and to the game itself.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/18/movies/magnus-review.html', 'snippet': 'Even fans of chess might struggle to connect with the cursory approach to this documentary’s subject, the prodigy Magnus Carlsen, and to the game itself.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There’s something ineffably sad about child prodigies. Tramping along an often solitary path to greatness, one that allows few detours for the innocent pleasures of childhood, these small wonders can tug at the heart. And so it is with Magnus Carlsen, the Norwegian chess champion at the center of “Magnus,” Benjamin Ree’s insipid and uninformative portrait of singularity and obsession.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/18/arts/18MAGNUS/18MAGNUS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 847, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/18/arts/18MAGNUS/18MAGNUS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 847}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/18/arts/18MAGNUS/18MAGNUS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 726, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/18/arts/18MAGNUS/18MAGNUS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1696, 'width': 1202, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/18/arts/