che Wiesen', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Moonglow (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-18T18:49:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6b63d002-55db-5deb-9132-d720df91318a', 'word_count': 525, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6b63d002-55db-5deb-9132-d720df91318a'}, {'abstract': 'For a supposedly neglected cohort, the white working class sure has made its presence felt lately on the best-seller list.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/27/books/review/the-story-behind-this-weeks-best-sellers.html', 'snippet': 'For a supposedly neglected cohort, the white working class sure has made its presence felt lately on the best-seller list.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bloc Party: For a supposedly neglected cohort, the white working class sure has made its presence felt lately on the best-seller list. (And in, you know, the voting booth. But this column is about books, not politics, so let’s take a deep breath and open our chakras; you can turn to the Op-Ed page if you really want to get riled up again.) This year the historian Nancy Isenberg spent 11 weeks on the hardcover nonfiction list with “White Trash,” tracing the role of poor white people from colonial times to the present. Then came J.D. Vance with “Hillbilly Elegy,” a blend of memoir and sociology that’s still selling well — after 16 weeks, it’s No.\u202f3 in hardcover nonfiction. Now here’s Arlie Russell Hochschild, a Berkeley sociologist who got to know Tea Party types in Louisiana for the National Book Award finalist “Strangers in Their Own Land,” new at No.\u202f14 on the hardcover nonfiction list.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image',