on': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/78f821e8-f714-572c-81be-b330e248b405', 'word_count': 993, 'uri': 'nyt://article/78f821e8-f714-572c-81be-b330e248b405'}, {'abstract': 'A group of foreign reformers was not able to change things, but the issue of officials getting rich at the public’s expense is still front and center.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/19/opinion/ukraines-imported-corruption-busters.html', 'snippet': 'A group of foreign reformers was not able to change things, but the issue of officials getting rich at the public’s expense is still front and center.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The whole saga of Mikheil Saakashvili, the former president of Georgia, showing up in Ukraine as the corruption-fighting governor of Odessa has been a curious one, as was his resignation last week with a loud flurry of attacks  on the resistance of the country’s leadership to reform. He also announced that he was forming a new Ukrainian political party. Setting aside Mr. Saakashvili’s political ambitions, the episode once again cast light on the tenacity of Ukraine’s culture of corruption.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/19/opinion/19sat3web/19sat3web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 396, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/19/opinion/19sat3web/19sat3web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 396}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/19/opinion/19sat3web/19sat3web-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 676, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/19/opinion/19sat3web/19sat3web-superJu