', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-20T21:38:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1dec9c97-0202-56a2-9a42-ad10793754a8', 'word_count': 198, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1dec9c97-0202-56a2-9a42-ad10793754a8'}, {'abstract': 'The new American foreign policy seems to be to tell allies they “should go and love yourself.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/21/opinion/donald-trump-and-the-bieber-doctrine.html', 'snippet': 'The new American foreign policy seems to be to tell allies they “should go and love yourself.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sofia, Bulgaria — It seems no matter what happens with American politics, the 19th-century French thinker Alexis de Tocqueville has something to say about it. In his classic “Democracy in America,” he suggested that democratic politics was in need of drama; as an election nears, “intrigues become more active, agitation more lively and more widespread. The entire nation falls into a feverish state.” But, once the election has passed, “everything becomes calm, and the river, one moment overflowed, returns peacefully to its bed.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/21/opinion/sunday/21krastev2-inyt/21krastev2-inyt-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/21/opinion/sunday/21krastev2-inyt/21krastev2-inyt-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/21/opinion/sunday/21krastev2-inyt/21krastev2-inyt-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy