ec4a5'}, {'abstract': 'For one sequence, the show offers a taste of the delicious (and fun) entertainment that might have been.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/21/arts/television/westworld-season-1-episode-8-recap.html', 'snippet': 'For one sequence, the show offers a taste of the delicious (and fun) entertainment that might have been.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With the few fragments of brain we have left this deep into the first season of “Westworld,” let us try once again to remember the source of Jonathan Nolan and Lisa Joy’s series, the starting point of the maze in which we currently find ourselves. Because for as much as Michael Crichton tried to suggest the dangers of technology and the hubris of resurrecting a world lost to history, his original “Westworld” was also a diverting piece of genre entertainment. Mr. Crichton always wanted to advance big, alarmist ideas — about viral plagues, about Japanese business tactics, about the true nature of sexual harassment — but his instinct for pleasing his audience is why he had a career as a best-selling author, rather than another penniless purveyor of hard sci-fi.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/21/arts/television/21westworld/21westworld-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/21/arts/television/21westworld/21westworld-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/21/arts/television/21westworld/21westworld-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/21/arts/television/21west