: 'Joe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Coscarelli', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/64f14f45-4576-576e-8f8d-31f9b167b148', 'word_count': 253, 'uri': 'nyt://article/64f14f45-4576-576e-8f8d-31f9b167b148'}, {'abstract': 'The latest outpost of Marco Canora’s bone broth chain offers flavor customization and frozen broths for home use.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/21/dining/bone-broth-brodo-west-village-marco-canora.html', 'snippet': 'The latest outpost of Marco Canora’s bone broth chain offers flavor customization and frozen broths for home use.', 'lead_paragraph': 'No doubt about it, Marco Canora really started something when he opened his first Brodo takeout window at Hearth, his East Village restaurant. That was two years ago, and now bone broth, spruced up with the Brodo label, is everywhere. Mr. Canora has expanded with a kiosk in Bryant Park, and he’s just opened a shop in the West Village where some basic broths are mixed and sometimes frothed with freshly milled herbs, Asian condiments, bone marrow, seaweed, butter and even cocoa. There are 11 preset seasoning combinations, $6 to $11, though customers can devise their own. Tom Yum, a combo with chicken broth, coconut milk, curry and chile oil, is becoming a favorite. The shop also sells basic broths, frozen, for home use: Brodo, 496 Hudson Street (Christopher Street), 212-366-0600, brodo.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/23/dining/23BURNER-BRODO/23BURNER-BRODO-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/23/dining/23BURNER-BRODO/23BURNER-BRODO-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0,