ect', 'value': 'Coaches and Managers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Arena, Bruce', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ARENA, BRUCE', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-21T20:12:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Borden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Borden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1f15451b-a053-548a-b02a-f2538ac95920', 'word_count': 1132, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1f15451b-a053-548a-b02a-f2538ac95920'}, {'abstract': 'Ancolie serves breakfast fare, layered salads and desserts in returnable glass jars.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/21/dining/breakfast-lunch-dessert-jars-ancolie.html', 'snippet': 'Ancolie serves breakfast fare, layered salads and desserts in returnable glass jars.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Food served in jars is not new, but Chloe Vichot, who fled finance for food and studied at culinary schools in New York along the way, has opened a shop dedicated exclusively to it. Ms. Vichot prepares and sells breakfast fare, bright lunch salads, soups, stews and desserts in glass jars, $5 to $14. Her specially made wide-mouth containers are more convenient than the usual Mason jars that some restaurants use to serve pâtés, desserts or even cocktails. The idea is to cut down on trash, and customers get a $1 discount for each jar they return (there is no jar deposit). The salads, in compact layers, are generous 22-ounce portions: Ancolie, 58 West Eighth Street (Avenue of the Americas), 646-524-5929, ancolie.co. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/23/dining/23BURNER-JAR/23BURNER-JAR-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/23/dining/23BURNER-JAR/23BURNER-JAR-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/23/dining/23BURNER-JAR/23BURNER-JAR-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/23/dining/23BURNER-JAR/23BURNER-JAR-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1536, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/23/dining/23BURNER-JAR/23BURNER-JAR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/23/dining/23BURNER-JAR/23BURNER-JAR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/23/dining/23BURNER-JAR/23BURNER-JAR-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Breakfast, Lunch and Dessert in Jars', 'kicker': 'Front Burner', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Spoon: Lunch Becomes a Layered Affair', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Salads', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ancolie (Manhattan, NY, Restaurant)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vichot, Chloe', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-21T20:21:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7bee9d1b-735b-5d98-9704-a5855e5fafb1', 'word_count': 122, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7bee9d1b-735b-5d98-9704-a5855e5fafb1'}, {'abstract': 'How he fought to preserve Alabama’s long history of separate and unequal education.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/21/opinion/jeff-sessions-other-civil-rights-problem.html', 'snippet': 'How he fought to preserve Alabama’s long history of separate and unequal education.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1986, the Republican-dominated Senate Judiciary Committee torpedoed Ronald Reagan’s nomination of Jeff Sessions to the federal bench. As sworn testimony there revealed, Mr. Sessions, then the United States attorney for the Southern District of Alabama, had referred to the N.A.A.C.P. and the Southern Christian Leadership Conference (founded by the Rev. Dr. Martin Luther King Jr.) as “un-American” and “Communist inspired.” He had joked that he thought the Ku Klux Klan was “O.K.” until he discovered some of its members smoked pot, and had accused a white attorney who supported voting rights of being a race traitor.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/22/opinion/21sugrueWeb/21sugrueWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 396, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/22/opinion/21sugrueWeb/21sugrueWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 396}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/22/opinion/21sugrueWeb/21sugrueWeb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 675, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/22/opinion/21sugrueWeb/21sugrueWeb-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1351, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/22/opinion/21sugrueWeb/21sugrueWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/22/opinion/21sugrueWeb/21sugrueWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/22/opinion/21sugrueWeb/21sugrueWeb-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Jeff Sessions’ Other Civil Rights Problem', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Attorneys General', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Segregation and Desegregation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Justice Department', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sessions, Jefferson B III', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ALABAMA', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-21T20:23:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas J. Sugrue', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Sugrue', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/1ed253e9-0d69-5ac4-847a-a594440091cf', 'word_count': 872, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1ed253e9-0d69-5ac4-847a-a594440091cf'}, {'abstract': 'Cured features the writers Betty Fussell, Jessica B. Harris, William Woys Weaver and Fuchsia Dunlop.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/21/dining/cured-magazine-food-preservation.html', 'snippet': 'Cured features the writers Betty Fussell, Jessica B. Harris, William Woys Weaver and Fuchsia Dunlop.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Cured, a periodical about preserving food, is definitely a niche publication. An impressive roster of writers, including Betty Fussell, Jessica B. Harris, William Woys Weaver and Fuchsia Dunlop, covers a wealth of topics under the editorial direction of Darra Goldstein, an author and a founder of Gastronomica. Learn about a Swedish tradition of fermenting fish that dates back 9,000 years; making persimmon vinegar; the allure of stinky Chinese food; pickles in the Nishiki Market in Kyoto, Japan; and zur, a fermented Polish rye soup. As Ms. Goldstein put it, preserving is as important a development for humankind as cooking, a means of “hedging our bets against hunger.” The magazine is published by Zero Point Zero, the company that produces “The Mind of a Chef” and Anthony Bourdain’s “Parts Unknown”: Cured, $20 at some food shops, bookstores nationwide and from curedmagazine.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/23/dining/23BURNER-BOOK/23BURNER-BOOK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 760, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/23/dining/23BURNER-BOOK/23BURNER-BOOK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 760}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/23/dining/23BURNER-BOOK/