ad of Fake News', 'kicker': 'Room for Debate', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2016-11-22T08:58:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c09167fa-964c-50e0-abbc-e836efb77c20', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c09167fa-964c-50e0-abbc-e836efb77c20'}, {'abstract': 'A new book features the photography of Jack London, along with his words, chronicling poverty in London and the aftermath of the San Francisco earthquake.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2016/11/22/rarely-seen-photos-by-jack-london/', 'snippet': 'A new book features the photography of Jack London, along with his words, chronicling poverty in London and the aftermath of the San Francisco earthquake.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One hundred years ago today, Jack London died in Sonoma County, Calif., at the age of 40. He was famous for novels, like the “The Call of the Wild,” which were based on his adventures, trekking through the Klondike or sailing the South Pacific. He was the archetype of the macho writer — before Ernest Hemingway — having been, among other things, a Socialist, a hobo, a sailor, a war correspondent and an oyster pirate.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rarely Seen Photos by Jack London', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Earthquakes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'London, Jack', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'San Francisco (Calif)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-22T09:00:41+00