 'word_count': 1111, 'uri': 'nyt://article/244da0a0-8b9b-5681-a478-bd24d7358f2f'}, {'abstract': 'His meeting with The Times was a marvel of boasting and bending.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/22/opinion/donald-trumps-demand-for-love.html', 'snippet': 'His meeting with The Times was a marvel of boasting and bending.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I had just shaken the president-elect’s normal-size hand and he was moving on to the next person when he wheeled around, took a half step back, touched my arm and looked me in the eye anew.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/23/opinion/23bruniWeb/23bruniWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/23/opinion/23bruniWeb/23bruniWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/23/opinion/23bruniWeb/23bruniWeb-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/23/opinion/23bruniWeb/23bruniWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/23/opinion/23bruniWeb/23bruniWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/23/opinion/23bruniWeb/23bruniWeb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/23/opinion/23bruniWeb/23bruniWeb-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Donald Trump’s Demand for Love', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Columnist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Trump’s Demand for Love', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-23T00:13:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Frank Bruni', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Frank', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bruni', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/2953c394-2f6e-5638-9483-507f9ab03c79', 'word_count': 797, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2953c394-2f6e-5638-9483-507f9ab03c79'}, {'abstract': 'Wherever we fall on the political spectrum, we’re going to see our desires and fears reflected in the art we encounter, especially in theater.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/22/theater/hamilton-theater-art-through-the-prism-of-changing-times.html', 'snippet': 'Wherever we fall on the political spectrum, we’re going to see our desires and fears reflected in the art we encounter, especially in theater.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last week, overcome by anxieties about the election results, I kept hearing variations on the same phrase — at the food co-op, in the lobbies of theaters, during an interview with an actor. Great art will come out of this, people told me. Times like this make for great art.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/27/arts/27SOLOSKI1/27SOLOSKI1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/27/arts/27SOLOSKI1/27SOLOSKI1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/27/arts/27SOLOSKI1/27SOLOSKI1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/27/arts/27SOLOSKI1/27SOLOSKI1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 971, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/27/arts/27SOLOSKI1/27SOLOSKI1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 971}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/27/arts/27SOLOSKI1/27SOLOSKI1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 633, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/27/arts/27SOLOSKI1/27SOLOSKI1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1265, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Watching Postelection, So Much Feels Different', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Watching Postelection, So Much Feels Different', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2016', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Natasha, Pierre & the Great Comet of 1812 (Play)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Public Theater', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-23T00:15:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexis Soloski', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexis', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Soloski', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/de11993b-a49f-5158-9561-aa4c953fb1c3', 'word_count': 952, 'uri': 'nyt://article/de11993b-a49f-5158-9561-aa4c953fb1c3'}, {'abstract': 'Attending the theater before 1850 or so in the United States was far more akin to attending a football game today than to attending contemporary theater.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/22/theater/hamilton-and-history.html', 'snippet': 'Attending the theater before 1850 or so in the United States was far more akin to attending a football game today than to attending contemporary theater.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1832, during a performance of “Richard III” at the Bowery Theater in Manhattan, 300 boisterous audience members joined the cast onstage to participate in the final sword fight and the slaying of the tyrannical King Richard. According to a newspaper account, “the audience mingled with the soldiers and raced across the stage” and during the last duel “made a ring around the combatants to see fair play, and kept them at it for nearly a quarter of an hour.” In today’s theater, where audience members are admonished to unwrap their candy in advance to avoid making a sound, such a scene is hard to fathom; in the 19th-century theater, it was of a piece (although on the rowdy side of the spectrum) with standard audience behavior.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/27/arts/27DILLON1/27DILLON1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/27/arts/27DILLON1/27DILLON1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/27/arts/27DILLON1/27DILLON1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/27/arts/27DILLON1/27DILLON1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 504, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/27/arts/27DILLON1/27DILLON1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 504}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/27/arts/27DILLON1/27DILLON1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 861, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/27/arts/27DILLON1/27DILLON1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1290, 'width': 1535, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Long Before ‘Hamilton’ Brouhaha, Theater Was Anything but Polite', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Speaking to, and From, the Stage', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dixon, Brandon Victor', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Irving, Washington', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pence, Mike', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Hamilton (Pla