11/23/magazine/in-vitro.html', 'snippet': 'Selected by Matthew Zapruder.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Aristotle wrote that poets are those who have “an eye for resemblances,” unexpected likenesses that only by means of the poem reveal themselves to us as true. This poet sees such an unexpected resemblance between medical forms and the scalp of an “unreal child,” presumably a desired one, and through that resemblance, he is able to conjure the mixture of feelings a person might have in this awkward, hopeful, itself quite unreal-seeming situation. Selected by Matthew Zapruder.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/27/magazine/27poem/27mag-27poem-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/27/magazine/27poem/27mag-27poem-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/27/magazine/27poem/27mag-27poem-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/27/magazine/27poem/27mag-27poem-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 275, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/27/magazine/27poem/27mag-27poem-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 275}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/27/magazine/27poem/27mag-27poem-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 470, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/