e': {'original': 'By Alexandra S. Levine', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexandra', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Levine', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'briefing', '_id': 'nyt://article/163e2a8b-e7e0-5d75-9b3c-e1fafbcb6fa1', 'word_count': 925, 'uri': 'nyt://article/163e2a8b-e7e0-5d75-9b3c-e1fafbcb6fa1'}, {'abstract': 'Make sure it has just your essentials (most people carry too many credit cards), and take the time to back up your personal documents.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/23/travel/safety-taking-your-wallet-on-vacation.html', 'snippet': 'Make sure it has just your essentials (most people carry too many credit cards), and take the time to back up your personal documents.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Losing your wallet or having it stolen is a nuisance anytime, but when you’re on vacation, it can ruin the hope of a good trip, according to Chris McGoey, a security consultant who has traveled to more than 110 countries. “People carry critical personal information like credit cards and a driver’s license in their wallet, and a lost or stolen one is a bigger headache and harder to recover from when you’re traveling,” he said.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/27/travel/27TRANSITTIPS/27TRANSITTIPS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/27/travel/27TRANSITTIPS/27TRANSITTIPS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/27/travel/27TRANSITTIPS/27TRANSITTIPS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJum