one elects the British head of state — no one but God. The monarch’s role is to be (or, at least, to appear) immutable. Queen Elizabeth II wields a delicate, unsought sovereignty that can never be eschewed (except by abdication or death) and is rarely exercised. She must evolve with change and sometimes nudge it — but never, ever demand it.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/23/watching/23crown1/23crown1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/23/watching/23crown1/23crown1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/23/watching/23crown1/23crown1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/23/watching/23crown1/23crown1-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1362, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/23/watching/23crown1/23crown1-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/23/watching/23crown1/23crown1-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/23/watching/23crown1/23crown1-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Tina Brown on the Final Two Episodes of ‘The Crown’: Love and Duty', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Crown (TV Program)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Netflix Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-23T15:03:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tina Brown', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brown', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1a51e8e0-66da-5d37-a7c4-a959640dd429', 'word_count': 1175, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1a51e8e0-66da-5d37-a7c4-a959640dd429'}, {'abstract': 'Latin America’s ideological pendulum is shifting from left to right, but new leaders are also repeating the errors of their predecessors.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/23/opinion/the-curse-of-hypercorrection-in-latin-america.html', 'snippet': 'Latin America’s ideological pendulum is shifting from left to right, but new leaders are also repeating the errors of their predecessors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BOGOTÁ, Colombia — In the late 2000s, leftist politicians were leading countries all across Latin America. Today, most are in retreat, challenged by a new crop of would-be leaders promising to fix the problems created by the left. In Argentina, Paraguay and Peru, these challengers have won elections. In Brazil, they took office this year as a result of a presidential impeachment and, as in Chile, gained ground in municipal elections. In Colombia, Mexico and Venezuela, they are regaining electoral strength.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/24/opinion/24corrales-web/24corrales-inyt-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/24/opinion/24corrales-web/24corrales-inyt-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/24/opinion/24corrales-web/24corrales-inyt-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/24/opinion/24corrales-web/24corrales-inyt-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 421, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/24/opinion/24corrales-web/24corrales-inyt-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 421}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/24/opinion/24corrales-web/24corrales-inyt-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 719, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/24/opinion/24corrales-web/24corrales-inyt-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1438, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Curse of Hypercorrection in Latin America', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Latin America’s curse of hypercorrection', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Trade and World Market', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Colombia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-23T15:27:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Javier Corrales', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Javier', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Corrales', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/63b59999-0ce9-57fd-8128-28a6cb7d7790', 'word_count': 1478, 'uri': 'nyt://article/63b59999-0ce9-57fd-8128-28a6cb7d7790'}, {'abstract': 'From the archives of The International Herald Tribune: Lawmakers in the French Parliament disagree on a labor law limiting the daily hours of female workers in November 1891.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2016/11/23/1891-a-10-hour-day-for-women-in-france/', 'snippet': 'From the archives of The International Herald Tribune: Lawmakers in the French Parliament disagree on a labor law limiting the daily hours of female workers in November 1891.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is a prospect of a conflict between the two French Chambers in respect to the new law for regulating labor. The Bill as it left the Chamber provided that ten hours per day should be the maximum for youths under eighteen, boys, girls, and women of all ages. When the law reached the Senate there was an animated discussion of the portion of the clause relating to women; and eventually, on the motion of M. Berenger, the words “and women” were struck out by 109 votes to 99.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '1891: A 10-Hour Day for Women in France', 'kicker': 'IHT Retrospective', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Legislatures and Parliaments', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-23T15:59:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By International Herald Tribune', 'person': [{'firstname': 'International', 'middlename': 'Herald', 'lastname': 'Tribune', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c7796ed5-33bf-5a6a-a0d7-20910ac05166', 'word_count': 247, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c7796ed5-33bf-5a6a-a0d7-20910ac05166'}, {'abstract': 'The recent successful sales of Impressionist, modern and contemporary art in New York showed that auction houses are now attracting blue-chip artworks, albeit with financial inducements.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/23/arts/design/sothebys-christies-and-phillips-take-different-paths-to-sold.html', 'snippet': 'The recent successful sales of Impressionist, modern and contemporary art in New York showed that auction houses are now attracting blue-chip artworks, albeit with financial inducements.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was difficult to escape a sense of unreality in Manhattan last week.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/26/arts/26REYBURN01-INYT/26REYBURN01-INYT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/26/arts/26REYBURN01-INYT/26REYBURN01-INYT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/26/arts/26REYBURN01-INYT/26REYBURN01-INYT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 