imes.com/2016/11/23/arts/design/looking-back-on-a-revolution-russian-art-at-moma.html', 'snippet': 'MoMA will unveil “A Revolutionary Impulse: The Rise of the Russian Avant-Garde,” a culling of nearly 270 works from its extensive holdings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Museum of Modern Art is getting a head start celebrating the centennial of the Russian Revolution of 1917. On Saturday, Dec. 3, it will unveil “A Revolutionary Impulse: The Rise of the Russian Avant-Garde,” a culling of nearly 270 works from its extensive holdings. Everything on view was made between 1912, when European modernism was especially influential among a talented cadre of Russian artists, and 1934, when the Soviet government clamped down on avant-garde activities. The show will put on view nearly all of the museum’s Russian paintings and sculptures from these years, tracing the absorption of Cubism and Futurism and the rise of Suprematism and Constructivism. There will also be films and film posters, photographs, illustrated books, prints, drawings, utilitarian objects and magazines, including a complete run of 22 issues of Novyi Lef, or New Left, magazine, recently acquired, which reflect the design and photographic genius of Aleksandr Rodchenko. (Through March 12; moma.org.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/27/arts/27WEEKAHEAD1/27WEEKAHEAD1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/27/arts/27WEEKAHEAD1/27WEEKAHEAD1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/27/arts/27WEEKAHEAD1/27WEEKAHEAD1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, 