T00:23:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steve Eder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eder', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5c222111-04a0-579d-a623-ed33367f1f89', 'word_count': 1294, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5c222111-04a0-579d-a623-ed33367f1f89'}, {'abstract': 'At a bunkerlike space in Manhattan, workers and volunteers have tended to 3,500 strays and orphans. At the city’s shelters, kitten euthanasias are down by more than 20 percent.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/23/nyregion/aspca-kitten-nursery.html', 'snippet': 'At a bunkerlike space in Manhattan, workers and volunteers have tended to 3,500 strays and orphans. At the city’s shelters, kitten euthanasias are down by more than 20 percent.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When you’re a kitten, there’s a lot to learn. In a room lined with cages on the Upper East Side of Manhattan one recent afternoon, Imando was getting a lesson in eating solid food.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Two Rooms, 300 Kittens', 'kicker': 'Pet City', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Parenting Help in ‘Kitten Season’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN SOCIETY FOR THE PREVENTION OF CRUELTY TO ANIMALS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Upper East Side (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cats', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Animal Abuse, Rights and Welfare', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pets', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-24T00:25:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metropolitan', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Newman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Newman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8bc1bb96-cf2c-525e-b43b-974ee51ea851', 'word_count': 827, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8bc1bb96-cf2c-525e-b43b-974ee51ea851'}, {'abstract': 'The community center dedicated to Jewish life offers a gym, a prestigious nursery, a vast array of classes, and is one of New York City’s premier cultural institutions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/23/nyregion/92nd-street-y.html', 'snippet': 'The community center dedicated to Jewish life offers a gym, a prestigious nursery, a vast array of classes, and is one of New York City’s premier cultural institutions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Looming above Lexington Avenue, the 92nd Street Y is one of the premier cultural institutions in New York City. It was here that Emma Lazarus taught English to immigrants; here that the Nobel Prize winner Elie Wiesel gave 180 lectures; here that the cellist Yo-Yo Ma made his New York City debut; here that the choreographer Alvin Ailey first presented “Revelations” and that the writer Truman Capote introduced “In Cold Blood.” It is also a great place to swim, throw a clay pot and hear a talk about the women of the Supreme Court.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/22/nyregion/27joint1/27joint1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/22/nyregion/27joint1/27joint1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, '