4d81'}, {'abstract': 'This book by Arthur and Barbara Gelb expresses a professional admiration for Mr. O’Neill but goes into great detail about the unflattering parts of his life.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/27/books/review-by-women-possessed-a-remix-of-a-flawed-eugene-oneill.html', 'snippet': 'This book by Arthur and Barbara Gelb expresses a professional admiration for Mr. O’Neill but goes into great detail about the unflattering parts of his life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The night that Eugene O’Neill’s five-hour drama “Strange Interlude” opened on Broadway in 1928, the anxious playwright stayed away. He sent in his stead not his wife, Agnes, but a woman with whom he had lately grown obsessed and for whom he would soon abandon Agnes — the glamorous actress Carlotta Monterey.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/28/arts/28BOOKGELB3/28BOOKGELB3-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/28/arts/28BOOKGELB3/28BOOKGELB3-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/28/arts/28BOOKGELB3/28BOOKGELB3-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/28/arts/28BOOKGELB3/28BOOKGELB3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 508, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/28/arts/28BOOKGELB3/28BOOKGELB3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 508}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images