 GABLES, Fla. — Juanita Castro considered her brother Fidel a traitor and had not spoken to him in 52 years, but, even so, she feels that a piece of her is missing now that he is dead.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/28/us/29JUANITA1-web/29JUANITA1-web-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/28/us/29JUANITA1-web/29JUANITA1-web-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/28/us/29JUANITA1-web/29JUANITA1-web-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/28/us/29JUANITA1-web/29JUANITA1-web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 403, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/28/us/29JUANITA1-web/29JUANITA1-web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 403}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/28/us/29JUANITA1-web/29JUANITA1-web-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 687, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/28/us/29JUANITA1-web/29JUANITA1-web-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1375, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Fidel Castro’s Sister, an Outspoken Critic, Takes No Joy in His Death', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Estranged Sister in Florida, an Outspoken Critic, Takes No Joy in Castro’s Death', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Robles, Frances', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Castro, Fidel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Castro, Juanita', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cuba', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Coral Gables (Fla)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Miami (Fla)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Florida', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-28T06:25:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Frances Robles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Frances', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Robles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5bdd7f08-33d1-57b2-9839-7f129b6411b7', 'word_count': 1018, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5bdd7f08-33d1-57b2-9839-7f129b6411b7'}, {'abstract': 'We left Cuba and our extended family behind in 1980. Nothing can bring those lost years back.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/28/opinion/what-castros-death-means-for-a-child-of-mariel.html', 'snippet': 'We left Cuba and our extended family behind in 1980. Nothing can bring those lost years back.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Growing up, I’d break into a cold sweat anytime the rumor circulated that Fidel Castro was dead. To me that meant returning to the island that was supposed to be home, but that I barely remembered. “I don’t know anyone there,” I’d think. “Where will I go to school? Where will we live?”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/28/opinion/28verdeWEB3/28verdeWEB3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/28/opinion/28verdeWEB3/28verdeWEB3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/28/opinion/28verdeWEB3/28verdeWEB3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 612, 'width': 612, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/28/opinion/28verdeWEB3/28verdeWEB3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 612, 'width': 612, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/28/opinion/28verdeWEB3/28verdeWEB3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/28/opinion/28verdeWEB3/28verdeWEB3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/28/opinion/28verdeWEB3/28verdeWEB3-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What Castro’s Death Means for a Child of Mariel', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Castro, Fidel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cuba', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Havana (Cuba)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Miami (Fla)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-28T06:48:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Isvett Verde', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Isvett', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Verde', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/f2450f85-9920-512a-ab67-e6819620b7f6', 'word_count': 854, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f2450f85-9920-512a-ab67-e6819620b7f6'}, {'abstract': 'Maeve continues to move forward while everyone else moves inward.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/28/arts/television/westworld-season-1-episode-9-recap.html', 'snippet': 'Maeve continues to move forward while everyone else moves inward.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Among other things, “Westworld” is a metaphor for the act of creation — which, in essence, makes it a show about its own creation. We may have been promised the same thing as the guests: an Old West adventure, loaded with the sex and violence that we’ve come to expect from an HBO show. But the show has devoted itself to defying those expectations at every turn, by making pleasure another component to be analyzed, like a cold body on a slab. “Westworld” has a story to tell, but it’s also about storytelling, constantly forcing us to recognize the falseness of the stories that are given to us and the stories that we tell ourselves. Somewhere at the bottom of it all is the truth — “the center of the maze,” to use the show’s expression — but not even that feels like terra firma at this point. It could be just another rug for the show’s creators to pull.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/28/arts/television/28westworld/28westworld-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/28/arts/television/28westworld/28westworld-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/28/arts/television/28westworld/28westworld-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/28/arts/television/28westworld/28westworld-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/28/arts/television/28westworld/28westworld-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/28/arts/television/28westworld/28westworld-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/28/arts/television/28westworld/28westworld-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1000, 'width': 1500, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Westworld’ Season 1, Episode 9: You Broke My Mind', 'kicker': 'Westworld', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Westworld (TV Program)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-28T07:10:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Tobias', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename