:', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Detecting Misinformation', 'kicker': 'Letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Detecting Misinformation', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-28T08:21:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/0746e371-1598-5f5f-89e7-932ffc7b9900', 'word_count': 180, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0746e371-1598-5f5f-89e7-932ffc7b9900'}, {'abstract': 'Mitch McConnell should help retirees who worked in a dangerous industry and deserve what was promised by contract.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/28/opinion/retired-coal-miners-losing-their-safety-net.html', 'snippet': 'Mitch McConnell should help retirees who worked in a dangerous industry and deserve what was promised by contract.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The troubled coal industry, which stirred such great political concern during the presidential campaign, is about to take on a decidedly human dimension in the Senate. A bipartisan push is underway there to rescue the failing pension and health benefits of thousands of retired union coal miners. Their benefit plans have become severely underfunded during the industry’s downturn, as coal companies declare bankruptcy and renege on obligations to workers at a time when there are fewer young miners to contribute support.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/28/opinion/28mon3web/28mon3web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/