: 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/18d9c675-e440-52f1-824e-b4102f59b938', 'word_count': 657, 'uri': 'nyt://article/18d9c675-e440-52f1-824e-b4102f59b938'}, {'abstract': 'Even as others deceive us, we deceive ourselves.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/28/opinion/fake-news-and-the-internet-shell-game.html', 'snippet': 'Even as others deceive us, we deceive ourselves.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Only a few days after the presidential election, the Oxford English Dictionary crowned its international word of the year: post-truth. The dictionary defined it as “relating to or denoting circumstances in which objective facts are less influential in shaping public opinion than appeals to emotion and personal belief.” To say that the term captured the zeitgeist of 2016 is a lexigraphical understatement. The word, the dictionary’s editors explained, had “gone from being a peripheral term to being a mainstay in political commentary.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/28/opinion/28stoneWeb/28stoneWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/28/opinion/28stoneWeb/28stoneWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/28/opinion/28stoneWeb/28stoneWeb-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/28/opinion/28stoneWeb/28stoneWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 391, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/28/opinion/28stoneWeb/28stoneWeb-articleLarge.jpg',