ename': None, 'lastname': 'Chan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a1c9fec5-b1fc-5d40-945c-4d55a9b0a1e9', 'word_count': 663, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a1c9fec5-b1fc-5d40-945c-4d55a9b0a1e9'}, {'abstract': 'A court thinks big to address poverty.', 'web_url': 'https://kristof.blogs.nytimes.com/2016/11/28/a-baby-court-offers-hope-for-families/', 'snippet': 'A court thinks big to address poverty.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a family court judge in Flint, Michigan, I see poverty flood my courtroom in a vicious cycle of cases in dependency, delinquency, criminality, and then back to dependency. The mantra in family court is that no one should lose their kids because they’re poor. The sad reality is that this happens all too often when generational poverty creates what Nicholas Kristof calls a “broken class.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/22/opinion/22kristof_blog2/22kristof_blog2-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/11/22/opinion/22kristof_blog2/22kristof_blog2-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/22/opinion/22kristof_blog2/22kristof_blog2-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/22/opinion/22kristof_blog2/22kristof_blog2-articleLarge.png', 'height': 411, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/11/22/opinion/22kristof_blog2/22kristof_blog2-articleLarge.png', 'xla