ation': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b15748e3-218b-5c33-b9b3-2c5ceb88358a', 'word_count': 747, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b15748e3-218b-5c33-b9b3-2c5ceb88358a'}, {'abstract': 'Using complex and proprietary optics, the Imago camera creates life-size portraits directly on photo paper. A second Imago is in the works for Beijing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/29/arts/design/taking-a-selfie-in-berlin-with-a-3-ton-camera.html', 'snippet': 'Using complex and proprietary optics, the Imago camera creates life-size portraits directly on photo paper. A second Imago is in the works for Beijing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Raymond Shi first encountered what he calls “the camera of truth” in spring 2015. On a business trip to Germany for his China-based company, an online photo lab, he detoured to Berlin to try out the Imago, a three-ton steel-and-canvas colossus that required him to climb into a brightly lit chamber to capture his image on life-size photo paper.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/01/arts/01imago01-inyt/01imago01-inyt-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/01/arts/01imago01-inyt/01imago01-inyt-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/01/arts/01imago01-inyt/01imago01-inyt-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/01/arts/01imago01-inyt/02imago01-inyt-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy'