ine': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear, Ron Nixon, Jennifer Steinhauer and Thomas Kaplan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8682cf35-dd68-528d-8be0-f06d2dcec019', 'word_count': 1812, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8682cf35-dd68-528d-8be0-f06d2dcec019'}, {'abstract': 'Conservative nationalists have won at the ballot box. But they are still struggling to put forward an intellectual agenda.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/29/opinion/does-indias-right-wing-have-any-ideas.html', 'snippet': 'Conservative nationalists have won at the ballot box. But they are still struggling to put forward an intellectual agenda.', 'lead_paragraph': 'GOA, India — The three places to which I am connected by birth, origin and marriage — Britain, India and the United States — have now experienced revolutions at the ballot box. In each, an election has revealed that liberal, globalized coastal elites stand at a tremendous remove from heartlands in open revolt. The revolt does not look to the left for inspiration but to the right. Make no mistake: “Liberal” and “left” are now said in the same breath as “corrupt establishment,” and those with torches and pitchforks are nativists, populists and nationalists of every stripe.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/06/opinion/aatish-taseer/aatish-taseer-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/03/06/opinion/aatish-taseer/aatish-taseer-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'i