, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zuma, Jacob G', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AFRICAN NATIONAL CONGRESS', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Africa', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-11-29T16:15:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Norimitsu Onishi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Norimitsu', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Onishi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c570794a-702a-57a0-8f46-da3cb0d61c6d', 'word_count': 464, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c570794a-702a-57a0-8f46-da3cb0d61c6d'}, {'abstract': 'New travel books include “The Timbuktu School for Nomads” and “The Humorless Ladies of Border Control.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/29/books/review/new-travel-books.html', 'snippet': 'New travel books include “The Timbuktu School for Nomads” and “The Humorless Ladies of Border Control.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Traveling can be complicated and nervous-making, yet for many of us the compulsion to be propelled across the map and renewed amid unfamiliar landscapes is irresistible. As Bill Bryson writes in his introduction to THE BEST AMERICAN TRAVEL WRITING 2016 (Mariner/Houghton Mifflin, paper, $14.95), which highlights two dozen locations across the globe, travel is “enriching, stimulating, seductive, pleasurable — but very often a touch compulsive, too.” For an example of all these qualities, consult Gretel Ehrlich’s ravishing, troubling essay about her perilous journeys by dog sled on the melting ice floes off Greenland’s coast. (“When I asked if we were going to die, he smiled and said, ‘Imaqa.’ Maybe.”) Yet some travelers do seem to have reined in their wanderlust, alert to geopolitical flux. Five years ago, I learned the prudence of such caution when friends emailed me in alarm from Damascus, where they had been lured by an article I’d written about the city’s beguiling old-town souk — and had landed just as Syria’s civil war began. My apologies! But not everyone is deterred by warfare or global warming. A batch of new books bridges the gap between the timid and the intrepid wanderer, showing that with planning, a little luck and a modicum of attention to headlines, the world’s most arresting sights, tastes and wonders can still be yours — whether you conduct your journey in the 50 states, overseas, from the sanctuary of an armchair or from the bobbing back of a camel.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/04/books/review/1204-BKS-Travel/1204-BKS-Travel-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/04/books/review/1204-BKS-Travel/1204-BKS-Travel-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/04/books/review/1204-BKS-Travel/1204-BKS-Travel-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/04/books/review/1204-BKS-Travel/1204-BKS-Travel-articleLarge-v2.gif', 'height': 428, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/12/04/books/review/1204-BKS-Travel/1204-BKS-Travel-articleLarge-v2.gif', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 428}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/04/books/review/1204-BKS-Travel/1204-BKS-Travel-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 731, 'width': 1024, '