ame': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Boehler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'briefing', '_id': 'nyt://article/bf3c154d-937d-52d4-96f1-5e18b9960e7b', 'word_count': 1107, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bf3c154d-937d-52d4-96f1-5e18b9960e7b'}, {'abstract': 'This tiny region, with its cobblestone streets, old Catholic churches and narrow alleyways, has an almost European feel to it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/30/travel/macau-region-china-coast-frugal.html', 'snippet': 'This tiny region, with its cobblestone streets, old Catholic churches and narrow alleyways, has an almost European feel to it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As we bumped along in the No. 25 bus on Estrada do Istmo, it was impossible not to notice the Venetian Macau, a mountain of steel and glass, shining in the distance in the afternoon sun. Opened in 2007, it’s home to one of the largest casinos on earth. And it’s not alone: Of the 10 biggest casinos in the world in 2014 (based on revenue), a staggering eight were in Macau, a tiny region on the southern coast of China, where over half a million people are packed into fewer than 12 square miles.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/04/travel/04FRUGAL1/04FRUGAL1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/04/travel/04FRUGAL1/04FRUGAL1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/04/travel/04FRUGAL1/04FRUGAL1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption