, 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/20169594-5d44-530e-b071-124003a99bb7', 'word_count': 155, 'uri': 'nyt://article/20169594-5d44-530e-b071-124003a99bb7'}, {'abstract': 'Jake Broder brings back a cult comedian in the solo show “His Royal Hipness Lord Buckley.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/30/theater/yeah-he-influenced-dylan-and-carlin.html', 'snippet': 'Jake Broder brings back a cult comedian in the solo show “His Royal Hipness Lord Buckley.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lord Buckley is a founding father of stand-up comedy who is often left out of history books. A contemporary of Lenny Bruce, he influenced everyone from George Carlin to Bob Dylan (who supposedly took the term “jingle-jangle” in “Mr. Tambourine Man” from a stand-up routine). His albums are filled with jazzy retellings of classic stories (“A Christmas Carol,” “The Raven”) or historical incidents (the Gettysburg Address) in eccentric slang that mixed aristocratic airs with beatnik styles. His improvisational performance was so strange and hard to categorize that it doomed him to be a cult comic. Ken Kesey once described him as “a secret thing that people passed under the table.”', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/04/arts/04GUIDE1/04GUIDE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/04/arts/04GUIDE1/04GUIDE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/04/arts/04GUIDE1/04GUIDE1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 1