icle', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amanda Hess', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amanda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hess', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ab91660d-49c4-54c3-9153-950534c95d53', 'word_count': 1308, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ab91660d-49c4-54c3-9153-950534c95d53'}, {'abstract': 'Paola Infante lives with her 4-year-old daughter, Geraliz, who has autism, and her 8-month-old son, Joshua, who needs heart surgery, in a small Manhattan apartment with the kitchen in the living room.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/30/nyregion/neediest-cases-fund-autism.html', 'snippet': 'Paola Infante lives with her 4-year-old daughter, Geraliz, who has autism, and her 8-month-old son, Joshua, who needs heart surgery, in a small Manhattan apartment with the kitchen in the living room.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In her cramped apartment in Upper Manhattan, Paola Infante cooked chicken and rice on a gas stove, stirring the meal as she kept an eye on her daughter, Geraliz, racing around the room. Each time the girl came near the flames and kitchen knives, Ms. Infante used her body to gently push her away.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/01/nyregion/01neediestinfante/01neediestinfante-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/12/01/nyregion/01neediestinfante/01neediestinfante-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/01/nyregion/01neediestinfante/01neediestinfante-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop